1
00:00:43,170 --> 00:00:44,935
Yeah, I know, I told him that.

2
00:00:45,410 --> 00:00:48,116
Yeah, well, I made it very clear.
I˙ll see you.

3
00:00:48,540 --> 00:00:49,879
Clive, I don˙t know how many times
I˙ve told you

4
00:00:49,880 --> 00:00:51,879
but I require you to read it,
that˙s what I pay you for.

5
00:00:51,880 --> 00:00:53,819
We need to get
the legal advice before...

6
00:00:53,820 --> 00:00:57,585
Why on earth would you say
that it is my fault when apparently...

7
00:00:57,690 --> 00:00:59,719
As soon as we get close to the deal,
you say it˙s no good at all.

8
00:00:59,720 --> 00:01:02,368
It was fine. They changed it.
They changed it.

9
00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:05,729
Well, where is he?
He˙s meant to stay with the car.

10
00:01:05,730 --> 00:01:09,319
- Well, perhaps he went to the loo.
- Betting shop, more like.

11
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:13,739
No, he˙s had too many chances,
that˙s it.

12
00:01:13,740 --> 00:01:17,439
- I˙ll drive myself right now.
- James, you no longer have a licence.

13
00:01:17,440 --> 00:01:19,793
Well, I can˙t take the bus,
can I, Clive?

14
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:23,300
(WOMAN CHATTERING ON PHONE)

15
00:01:23,650 --> 00:01:26,709
Look, Flo, I told you.
This is not up for discussion.

16
00:01:27,080 --> 00:01:30,375
I can˙t see you tonight.
Unless you want to be my driver.

17
00:01:31,590 --> 00:01:34,826
No, no, I˙m going to
a Downing Street bash with my wife.

18
00:01:34,860 --> 00:01:37,213
Yeah, don˙t give me
an argument about it.

19
00:01:40,600 --> 00:01:42,060
(HORNS BLARING)

20
00:01:42,330 --> 00:01:43,910
(TYRES SCREECHING)

21
00:01:48,840 --> 00:01:50,860
(PANTING)

22
00:01:52,940 --> 00:01:55,110
Oh, no. No.

23
00:02:25,170 --> 00:02:29,112
- Mr Justice Everard, the new Presider.
- Yes, we were alerted, sir.

24
00:02:29,810 --> 00:02:32,420
Your Lordship. My cases are in the back.

25
00:02:32,850 --> 00:02:33,850
Yes, My Lord.

26
00:02:34,620 --> 00:02:36,550
(CLOCK CHIMING)

27
00:02:55,940 --> 00:02:59,999
- Well, who˙s occupying this room?
- Mr Justice Deed is here, My Lord.

28
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:02,260
Well, I˙m the senior judge.

29
00:03:02,610 --> 00:03:04,809
Deed has no right
to take the best room for himself.

30
00:03:04,810 --> 00:03:07,319
His Lordship arrived yesterday,
Your Lordship.

31
00:03:07,320 --> 00:03:10,144
Well, this is very shoddy,
very shoddy behaviour.

32
00:03:10,490 --> 00:03:13,330
My Lord, shall I move Sir John˙s things?

33
00:03:13,820 --> 00:03:17,190
No, put my bags back in the car.
I˙m not staying.

34
00:03:23,460 --> 00:03:24,969
Thank you for attending me
on the circuit

35
00:03:24,970 --> 00:03:27,769
and agreeing that I should give
judgement by myself.

36
00:03:27,770 --> 00:03:31,182
I will post the full text
of the judgement on the internet.

37
00:03:32,610 --> 00:03:36,493
This is an appeal from a decision
of the Employment Appeal Tribunal

38
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:37,950
on a point of law,

39
00:03:38,210 --> 00:03:41,019
that the core of the case
is the question whether injuries caused

40
00:03:41,020 --> 00:03:42,980
to Sergeant Jake Bridges

41
00:03:43,320 --> 00:03:45,519
could be legally
due to the work he was asked to do

42
00:03:45,520 --> 00:03:48,285
by his employer,
the Chief Constable for Sussex.

43
00:03:48,320 --> 00:03:51,750
This court does not accept
the argument of the appellant.

44
00:03:52,290 --> 00:03:53,900
You can˙t be serious.

45
00:03:57,670 --> 00:04:00,299
Sergeant Bridges,
disappointing as this must be for you,

46
00:04:00,300 --> 00:04:03,418
you must know from your
experience as a police officer

47
00:04:03,440 --> 00:04:06,469
that you really mustn˙t
address the court in this way.

48
00:04:06,470 --> 00:04:08,882
And whilst the court
sympathises with you,

49
00:04:08,940 --> 00:04:11,630
the injuries cannot be seen in law

50
00:04:12,080 --> 00:04:13,819
as having been caused by the work.

51
00:04:13,820 --> 00:04:16,690
Working with computers gave me ME.

52
00:04:17,190 --> 00:04:19,449
DEED: Well, I will continue
reading the judgement.

53
00:04:19,450 --> 00:04:22,509
You have a copy
and can consider it with your lawyer.

54
00:04:22,620 --> 00:04:23,960
May I just ask,

55
00:04:24,260 --> 00:04:27,084
I assume the Sussex Police
are not seeking costs?

56
00:04:29,260 --> 00:04:31,250
- No, My Lord.
- Very well.

57
00:04:38,310 --> 00:04:40,239
This another health hazard,
do you think?

58
00:04:40,240 --> 00:04:41,550
I expect so, Judge.

59
00:04:43,340 --> 00:04:47,119
You˙ve got that horrible man who killed
those two little girls and their mother

60
00:04:47,120 --> 00:04:49,230
- next on your list.
- Coopl

61
00:04:49,620 --> 00:04:51,930
- It˙s alleged he did this.
- No.

62
00:04:52,320 --> 00:04:54,780
He was driving
while on the mobile phone.

63
00:04:55,220 --> 00:04:58,129
I pulled a lot of strings
to get you this case, Judge.

64
00:04:58,130 --> 00:05:00,899
I˙m sure that you˙ll give Mr Brooklands
just what he˙s got coming.

65
00:05:00,900 --> 00:05:03,606
You˙re outrageous.
Could have been an accident.

66
00:05:03,670 --> 00:05:05,010
Hi, Jo, it˙s me.

67
00:05:05,300 --> 00:05:07,599
Any word yet from
the Professional Conduct Committee?

68
00:05:07,600 --> 00:05:09,299
I˙ve been trying your phone all day,

69
00:05:09,300 --> 00:05:11,569
don˙t you pick up your messages
any more?

70
00:05:11,570 --> 00:05:13,240
And, um,

71
00:05:14,010 --> 00:05:15,775
would you like to have dinner?

72
00:05:23,990 --> 00:05:25,689
She˙s gorgeous. Where˙d you get her?

73
00:05:25,690 --> 00:05:27,390
Where do you think?

74
00:05:27,690 --> 00:05:31,689
No sooner had I got Rosie re-homed
with my sister, Charlie finds this one.

75
00:05:31,690 --> 00:05:34,690
- She a puppy?
- Well, she thinks she is. She˙s two.

76
00:05:34,760 --> 00:05:36,349
- What˙s she called?
- Mimi.

77
00:05:36,860 --> 00:05:39,200
- Hellol
- Mimi.

78
00:05:40,700 --> 00:05:44,569
- Hey, I could do with some of that.
- Oh, no, no, no. Mimi˙s far safer.

79
00:05:44,570 --> 00:05:48,079
My case could come up before the
Professional Conduct Committee anytime.

80
00:05:48,080 --> 00:05:49,370
Wake up, John.

81
00:05:49,610 --> 00:05:52,728
I˙m accused of having sex
with the judge I was before.

82
00:05:53,180 --> 00:05:54,710
Which judge is that, then?

83
00:05:54,720 --> 00:05:56,479
I don˙t think
you˙re taking this seriously.

84
00:05:56,480 --> 00:05:57,989
I think you˙re worrying about nothing.

85
00:05:57,990 --> 00:06:00,520
Fine for you.
Me they can take off the road.

86
00:06:01,220 --> 00:06:03,691
Aren˙t you taking a risk
coming here, then?

87
00:06:04,930 --> 00:06:06,160
I want help.

88
00:06:07,160 --> 00:06:11,199
I˙m up against your ex in a civil case
and I˙m feeling a bit out of my depth.

89
00:06:11,200 --> 00:06:13,129
I wouldn˙t let it worry you.
It˙ll never get to court.

90
00:06:13,130 --> 00:06:15,189
Not with George, she˙ll cut a deal.

91
00:06:15,200 --> 00:06:17,160
Her clients are One-Way PLC.

92
00:06:17,540 --> 00:06:20,060
Oh, that civil case.

93
00:06:20,480 --> 00:06:23,230
And they won˙t settle. If they do,

94
00:06:23,680 --> 00:06:27,049
there˙ll be a flood of claims from
other damaged mobile phone users.

95
00:06:27,050 --> 00:06:29,130
Why are you doing civil work?

96
00:06:29,480 --> 00:06:33,400
Oh, I defended my client˙s brother
on a drug charge.

97
00:06:34,060 --> 00:06:36,919
She˙s hoping I can perform
the same magic for her.

98
00:06:36,920 --> 00:06:41,260
Well, it˙s all good experience
if you want to be a judge.

99
00:06:42,800 --> 00:06:45,918
What stroke is this, Mr Petros?
Where˙s the defendant?

100
00:06:46,200 --> 00:06:49,200
My Lord, Mr Brooklands
is in a psychiatric hospital.

101
00:06:49,440 --> 00:06:52,660
The psychiatrists say
he˙s not fit to stand trial.

102
00:06:53,170 --> 00:06:55,347
Are you running a defence of insanity

103
00:06:55,480 --> 00:06:58,349
to a charge of causing death
by dangerous driving?

104
00:06:58,350 --> 00:07:00,879
No, My Lord, we˙re not arguing
Mr Brooklands was insane

105
00:07:00,880 --> 00:07:02,319
at the time of the accident,

106
00:07:02,320 --> 00:07:06,830
rather, his incoherent state of mind now
means he˙s not fit to stand trial.

107
00:07:07,520 --> 00:07:11,530
This case has attracted
a great deal of public concern

108
00:07:12,160 --> 00:07:13,620
and even more anger.

109
00:07:14,560 --> 00:07:16,259
I noticed from the committal papers

110
00:07:16,260 --> 00:07:20,169
you argued your client couldn˙t get
a fair trial anywhere in the country

111
00:07:20,170 --> 00:07:24,229
owing to the amount of hostile publicity
that this case has attracted.

112
00:07:24,310 --> 00:07:27,193
You˙ll get a fair trial from me,
I can assure you.

113
00:07:27,340 --> 00:07:29,839
PETROS: Events have rather
overtaken Mr Brooklands.

114
00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:33,076
I would direct Your Lordship
to the psychiatric reports.

115
00:07:34,680 --> 00:07:39,430
As set out under the
Criminal Procedure (Insanity) Act 1964,

116
00:07:40,590 --> 00:07:43,459
my client is incapable
of instructing either myself

117
00:07:43,460 --> 00:07:46,637
or his solicitors as to
what submission to put forward.

118
00:07:46,830 --> 00:07:49,830
Well, that must be very
worrying for you, Mr Petros.

119
00:07:51,500 --> 00:07:55,206
However, I˙m not prepared to
accept these reports at face value.

120
00:07:55,570 --> 00:07:57,440
Under the 1964 Act,

121
00:07:57,770 --> 00:08:00,439
if there is any doubt as to
a defendant˙s fitness to plead,

122
00:08:00,440 --> 00:08:03,579
and I˙m prepared to give Mr Brooklands
the benefit of the doubt,

123
00:08:03,580 --> 00:08:06,480
we must settle this
before any evidence is heard.

124
00:08:07,880 --> 00:08:11,998
Now, I want you here tomorrow morning
with your client and his doctors.

125
00:08:12,250 --> 00:08:15,719
We shall empanel a jury to give
a ruling as to his mental state.

126
00:08:15,720 --> 00:08:20,132
If their verdict is that he is sane,
we will then try him on the indictment.

127
00:08:20,830 --> 00:08:23,359
If the court decides
that he is unfit to plead,

128
00:08:23,360 --> 00:08:25,360
then under the Criminal Procedures

129
00:08:25,570 --> 00:08:28,970
(Insanity and Unfitness to Plead)
Act 1991,

130
00:08:29,740 --> 00:08:32,211
we shall conduct a trial
of the fact alone.

131
00:08:32,770 --> 00:08:35,640
- 10:00 tomorrow.
- With respect, My Lord,

132
00:08:36,340 --> 00:08:40,164
Mr Brooklands˙ doctors say
he˙s not fit enough to appear in court.

133
00:08:40,380 --> 00:08:43,419
Do they indicate what
the possible outcome will be if he does?

134
00:08:43,420 --> 00:08:46,140
Severe damage to his mind
is likely to result.

135
00:08:46,620 --> 00:08:49,489
Well, the contention is that
he˙s already severely mentally damaged.

136
00:08:49,490 --> 00:08:52,329
Further damage will be difficult
to detect, will it not?

137
00:08:52,330 --> 00:08:55,599
I want you here tomorrow morning
with your client and his doctors.

138
00:08:55,600 --> 00:08:57,399
I˙m sure he˙ll be in very capable hands.

139
00:08:57,400 --> 00:08:58,450
All rise.

140
00:08:58,800 --> 00:09:01,506
My Lord, clearly this case
should not be heard.

141
00:09:01,540 --> 00:09:03,369
Mr Brooklands is not well enough.

142
00:09:03,370 --> 00:09:05,569
I˙m glad you are taking
such an interest, Mr James,

143
00:09:05,570 --> 00:09:06,747
but it˙s on my list.

144
00:09:06,770 --> 00:09:08,579
Manipulated there
by your clerk, My Lord.

145
00:09:08,580 --> 00:09:12,179
Then it˙s fortunate for Mr Brooklands
that he has such a fair-minded judge

146
00:09:12,180 --> 00:09:14,879
who won˙t be influenced by
the size of his political donations.

147
00:09:14,880 --> 00:09:16,020
Excuse me, My Lord.

148
00:09:16,250 --> 00:09:19,489
Are you free for a directions
hearing in a negligence case?

149
00:09:19,490 --> 00:09:21,319
If it˙s not too complicated,
Mr Harrison.

150
00:09:21,320 --> 00:09:22,689
Oh, half a day, My Lord.

151
00:09:22,690 --> 00:09:26,455
The case itself concerns a claim
for damages against One-Way PLC.

152
00:09:26,560 --> 00:09:29,208
Mr Justice Everard
was to have heard it but...

153
00:09:29,460 --> 00:09:31,499
Well, counsel for both
parties are waiting.

154
00:09:31,500 --> 00:09:33,729
Why isn˙t Mr Justice Everard hearing it?

155
00:09:33,730 --> 00:09:35,839
Well, it appears he did arrive
at the lodgings, My Lord,

156
00:09:35,840 --> 00:09:37,605
but was called back to London.

157
00:09:37,910 --> 00:09:41,263
All right, I˙ll read the papers.
Tell counsel after lunch.

158
00:09:41,840 --> 00:09:43,664
You˙ve had my answer, Mr James.

159
00:09:46,010 --> 00:09:47,849
Have you heard who we˙re now before?

160
00:09:47,850 --> 00:09:49,719
Mr Justice Everard. It˙s on the list.

161
00:09:49,720 --> 00:09:52,850
Oh, no. It˙s the Deed.

162
00:09:53,390 --> 00:09:56,214
John? Well, that doesn˙t
exactly suit me, George.

163
00:09:56,420 --> 00:09:58,829
I could be hauled up in front
of the Professional Conduct Committee

164
00:09:58,830 --> 00:09:59,830
at any moment.

165
00:09:59,890 --> 00:10:02,520
So we all know which way
the case will go.

166
00:10:03,860 --> 00:10:05,569
We could make a joint approach

167
00:10:05,570 --> 00:10:07,869
and ask him to recuse himself
from the case

168
00:10:07,870 --> 00:10:09,939
because of his relationship with us.

169
00:10:09,940 --> 00:10:12,250
My relationship isn˙t the problem, Jo.

170
00:10:12,640 --> 00:10:15,809
- Your relationship is.
- We don˙t have a relationship.

171
00:10:15,810 --> 00:10:17,950
That˙s what I hope the PCC accept.

172
00:10:19,680 --> 00:10:21,050
You˙ll be lucky.

173
00:10:21,350 --> 00:10:23,821
I don˙t particularly
want to hear the case.

174
00:10:24,120 --> 00:10:26,170
Mr Justice Everard is indisposed.

175
00:10:26,520 --> 00:10:28,270
With his leg over some bit.

176
00:10:29,120 --> 00:10:31,729
Sorry, Mrs Channing, what was that?
I didn˙t quite...

177
00:10:31,730 --> 00:10:33,510
I said, "We˙ll miss his wit."

178
00:10:34,100 --> 00:10:36,453
The Court of Appeal laid down guidelines

179
00:10:36,930 --> 00:10:40,799
as to when a judge may recuse himself
from a case. None of them apply here.

180
00:10:40,800 --> 00:10:43,900
With your leave, Judge,
I˙m compelled to disagree.

181
00:10:44,410 --> 00:10:48,310
Ex-wives are fine,
but with current wives or lovers,

182
00:10:48,940 --> 00:10:51,079
there˙s a risk of possible pillow talk.

183
00:10:51,080 --> 00:10:52,949
You˙ve been listening
to the gossip, George.

184
00:10:52,950 --> 00:10:55,715
You know what they say,
"No smoke without fire."

185
00:10:55,720 --> 00:10:58,544
There˙s another saying,
"All gong and no dinner."

186
00:10:59,250 --> 00:11:01,721
Well, I can see I˙m going
to have such fun.

187
00:11:01,920 --> 00:11:03,700
There are remedies to redress

188
00:11:04,060 --> 00:11:07,884
any miscarriage you feel may follow
from my having heard the case.

189
00:11:08,960 --> 00:11:10,372
Now, can we get started?

190
00:11:10,770 --> 00:11:12,790
(BELL CHIMING)

191
00:11:18,970 --> 00:11:22,100
Ian. Mrs Channing just phoned me.

192
00:11:22,610 --> 00:11:25,830
- Deed is hearing the One-Way case.
- So it seems.

193
00:11:26,380 --> 00:11:28,600
It˙s a recipe for disaster.

194
00:11:29,020 --> 00:11:31,819
We˙re doing all we can
to retrieve the situation.

195
00:11:31,820 --> 00:11:33,519
But short of a bomb going off...

196
00:11:33,520 --> 00:11:35,489
Well, surely there˙s some way
we can head it off.

197
00:11:35,490 --> 00:11:37,920
It˙s already at court,
that˙s the problem.

198
00:11:38,330 --> 00:11:41,729
Mrs Channing will have any number
of delaying arguments at her disposal.

199
00:11:41,730 --> 00:11:43,460
Let˙s hope she uses them.

200
00:11:44,530 --> 00:11:47,140
Meanwhile, I suppose
the claimant might die.

201
00:11:47,570 --> 00:11:50,923
That would seem rather heartless,
even for the government.

202
00:11:51,140 --> 00:11:54,009
I will get an update
on her medical prognosis, Minister.

203
00:11:54,010 --> 00:11:56,599
I gather her brain tumour˙s
quite aggressive.

204
00:11:56,640 --> 00:11:58,950
Good. Keep me informed.

205
00:11:59,650 --> 00:12:02,062
What happened
down at Highfield, Laurence?

206
00:12:02,320 --> 00:12:04,900
If any case needs a reliable judge

207
00:12:05,320 --> 00:12:08,160
it˙s this negligence action
against One-Way.

208
00:12:08,660 --> 00:12:11,180
Mr Justice Everard is refusing to sit.

209
00:12:11,630 --> 00:12:16,219
It seems Mr Justice Deed arrived first
and took the best rooms at the lodgings.

210
00:12:17,600 --> 00:12:21,659
Is this what I am to tell the
Lord Chancellor if this case goes wrong?

211
00:12:22,040 --> 00:12:24,923
It will go wrong
under Deed˙s perverse management.

212
00:12:25,370 --> 00:12:28,209
- Get it away from him.
- Well, we don˙t see how, Sir Ian.

213
00:12:28,210 --> 00:12:29,879
There˙s a lot of bad feeling
between them.

214
00:12:29,880 --> 00:12:32,549
It came about
at the Human Rights Seminar at Warwick.

215
00:12:32,550 --> 00:12:36,139
Then putting them together
wasn˙t a sound management strategy.

216
00:12:36,520 --> 00:12:39,289
Why wasn˙t Deed sent to Carlisle
or somewhere instead?

217
00:12:39,290 --> 00:12:41,349
He wanted Highfield again, Sir Ian.

218
00:12:41,660 --> 00:12:44,366
You thought Mr Justice Everard
would check him.

219
00:12:44,630 --> 00:12:47,380
Then Everard must get back there
and check himl

220
00:12:47,860 --> 00:12:50,599
- Where is he?
- In chambers in the Strand, sir.

221
00:12:50,600 --> 00:12:53,469
- I better go and talk to him.
- Deed is due to start

222
00:12:53,470 --> 00:12:55,899
the directions hearing
after lunch adjournment.

223
00:12:55,900 --> 00:12:59,018
If he does, there˙s no way
we can prise the case away.

224
00:13:00,410 --> 00:13:03,220
We could always appeal
to his better nature.

225
00:13:03,680 --> 00:13:06,290
(CHUCKLES) Yes, why not?

226
00:13:11,620 --> 00:13:14,797
- Sorry to interrupt your lunch, John.
- Sit down, Ian.

227
00:13:19,860 --> 00:13:22,580
The matter is of some importance.

228
00:13:23,060 --> 00:13:26,220
It˙s vegetables and tomato ketchup.

229
00:13:28,300 --> 00:13:30,889
Best cook could come up with
at short notice.

230
00:13:30,970 --> 00:13:32,539
Didn˙t she know you were arriving?

231
00:13:32,540 --> 00:13:35,239
Yes, yes, I just forgot to tell her
I˙d become a vegetarian.

232
00:13:35,240 --> 00:13:37,609
Ah, that could make
official dining functions awkward.

233
00:13:37,610 --> 00:13:38,690
No, not really.

234
00:13:38,950 --> 00:13:41,774
I˙lljust get Mrs Cooper
to alert them in advance.

235
00:13:44,050 --> 00:13:46,345
- Your daughter˙s influence?
- Mmm, yes.

236
00:13:46,690 --> 00:13:49,970
But however, I did weigh the evidence
as I usually do.

237
00:13:50,490 --> 00:13:53,070
And came to your usual
perverse judgement.

238
00:13:54,700 --> 00:13:55,960
Yes.

239
00:13:56,500 --> 00:14:00,618
Now you˙re worried about a perverse
judgement in the mobile phone case.

240
00:14:01,870 --> 00:14:03,839
Have you taken up mind-reading, John?

241
00:14:03,840 --> 00:14:06,769
The more experience one gets
the more contacts one makes.

242
00:14:06,770 --> 00:14:09,009
I got an email from somebody
in your department.

243
00:14:09,010 --> 00:14:10,179
Would you mind telling me who?

244
00:14:10,180 --> 00:14:11,380
(LAUGHS)

245
00:14:12,080 --> 00:14:15,139
When we breed a culture
of spying and reporting back,

246
00:14:15,420 --> 00:14:17,380
eventually we get spied upon

247
00:14:18,620 --> 00:14:20,370
and reported back.

248
00:14:22,920 --> 00:14:26,359
It˙s thought less than desirable
your hearing the cell phone case

249
00:14:26,360 --> 00:14:28,030
with Mrs Mills involved,

250
00:14:28,330 --> 00:14:31,566
in view of her hearing
before the Disciplinary Tribunal.

251
00:14:32,100 --> 00:14:34,000
Is that still going ahead?

252
00:14:38,310 --> 00:14:41,740
If this cell phone case were to go
against the company

253
00:14:42,780 --> 00:14:45,949
- it could prove difficult for you?
- Not me personally.

254
00:14:45,950 --> 00:14:49,580
I mean, it could open
a floodgate of litigation.

255
00:14:51,190 --> 00:14:53,530
Like with tobacco and asbestos.

256
00:14:53,920 --> 00:14:57,489
We˙d regard it as a great favour
if you were to let it go to back to

257
00:14:57,490 --> 00:14:59,300
Monty Everard in the Strand.

258
00:14:59,660 --> 00:15:02,543
The claimant is very poorly
and she lives locally.

259
00:15:03,400 --> 00:15:06,753
It seems unkind to ask her to go
all the way up to London.

260
00:15:07,100 --> 00:15:08,470
On the other hand,

261
00:15:10,240 --> 00:15:11,939
being owed a favour by your lot

262
00:15:11,940 --> 00:15:14,646
might be a very good position
to put myself in.

263
00:15:15,440 --> 00:15:17,170
We would be grateful.

264
00:15:18,650 --> 00:15:20,960
Is Monty Everard amenable to pressure?

265
00:15:21,780 --> 00:15:22,780
Ah.

266
00:15:25,390 --> 00:15:29,019
Does that mean you˙re not interested
in what we might offer you?

267
00:15:29,020 --> 00:15:31,197
Just not convinced you could deliver.

268
00:15:31,230 --> 00:15:32,930
You˙d have my word.

269
00:15:34,900 --> 00:15:38,410
Then talk to me about my elevation
to the Appellate Bench.

270
00:15:40,770 --> 00:15:43,380
The Lord Chancellor
regrets not having a mind

271
00:15:43,800 --> 00:15:45,800
such as yours in the Appeal Court.

272
00:15:46,940 --> 00:15:52,450
And this ridiculously unfair PCC hearing
against Mrs Mills being dropped.

273
00:15:54,180 --> 00:15:57,945
The Lord Chancellor˙s department
had very little influence there.

274
00:15:59,120 --> 00:16:03,250
They˙re all reasonable men
on the Disciplinary Tribunal.

275
00:16:08,260 --> 00:16:12,437
Well, the only question remaining, then,
Ian, is can we achieve all this

276
00:16:12,930 --> 00:16:15,240
within the next 20 minutes?

277
00:16:16,540 --> 00:16:18,540
I hardly think that˙s practicable.

278
00:16:18,910 --> 00:16:21,839
Because that˙s when I˙m hearing
the arguments for directions

279
00:16:21,840 --> 00:16:23,890
in the mobile phone case.

280
00:16:25,650 --> 00:16:30,510
We respect the integrity and
independence of the judiciary, Sir John.

281
00:16:32,090 --> 00:16:34,796
But there comes a point
when its very existence

282
00:16:35,190 --> 00:16:38,650
depends upon a workable relationship
with the Executive.

283
00:16:39,860 --> 00:16:42,900
Never more so than in the case
you˙re about to hear.

284
00:16:43,400 --> 00:16:47,518
See, with the licence revenue from
the mobile phone companies at stake,

285
00:16:47,940 --> 00:16:52,510
well, what is it, ￡22 billion,
this workable relationship

286
00:16:53,210 --> 00:16:57,280
might easily deteriorate
into a master-servant relationship.

287
00:16:57,950 --> 00:16:59,090
Couldn˙t it, Ian?

288
00:17:01,820 --> 00:17:03,930
Sure you won˙t stay for lunch?

289
00:17:06,990 --> 00:17:08,859
Did you get anywhere with Monty Everard?

290
00:17:08,860 --> 00:17:12,260
No, sir, I˙m afraid not.
But one of our sources told us

291
00:17:12,830 --> 00:17:16,259
that Deed slept with an attractive
French judge at the Warwick seminar

292
00:17:16,260 --> 00:17:19,849
after Mr Justice Everard
had spent three days chatting her up.

293
00:17:20,170 --> 00:17:23,994
There˙s no longer time for Everard
to get here before Deed starts.

294
00:17:24,440 --> 00:17:25,900
I'll speak to Everard.

295
00:17:26,170 --> 00:17:29,879
Meanwhile, you better come up with some
delaying strategies and fast, Laurence.

296
00:17:29,880 --> 00:17:31,720
(FIRE ALARM RINGING)

297
00:17:40,620 --> 00:17:42,900
(MAN CHATTERING ON RADIO)

298
00:17:47,330 --> 00:17:51,020
- What˙s happening, Coop?
- It˙s a bomb scare, Judge.

299
00:17:51,600 --> 00:17:53,369
DEED: How long
are we going to be displaced?

300
00:17:53,370 --> 00:17:56,639
Well, the police are making
a minute search of all the buildings.

301
00:17:56,640 --> 00:17:58,405
That could take all afternoon.

302
00:17:58,610 --> 00:18:02,787
The court manager is advising judges
to send their cases home for today.

303
00:18:03,380 --> 00:18:04,990
Is he?

304
00:18:05,650 --> 00:18:09,520
Row, its John, I'm at court.
We've got a bomb scare.

305
00:18:10,120 --> 00:18:13,238
If I needed some information,
could you get it for me?

306
00:18:13,390 --> 00:18:15,743
I doubt if I˙d tell you,
even if I could.

307
00:18:16,020 --> 00:18:19,559
It˙d be available on a need-to-know
basis only. Why do you need to know?

308
00:18:19,560 --> 00:18:23,430
Oh, maybe I˙m just being paranoid.

309
00:18:24,060 --> 00:18:26,369
Listen, thanks, Row.
I˙ll call you later.

310
00:18:26,370 --> 00:18:28,139
Police don˙t know
where the warning came from

311
00:18:28,140 --> 00:18:30,339
but the caller had enough information
about the building

312
00:18:30,340 --> 00:18:32,240
for them to take it seriously.

313
00:18:32,570 --> 00:18:33,969
So the caller knew the building?

314
00:18:33,970 --> 00:18:35,520
(MIMI BARKING)

315
00:18:35,810 --> 00:18:37,239
Can I get in and get my papers?

316
00:18:37,240 --> 00:18:39,610
Everyone wants to do that, Judge.

317
00:18:40,010 --> 00:18:41,120
(EXHALES)

318
00:18:41,380 --> 00:18:43,079
Find Mrs Mills and Mrs Channing.

319
00:18:43,080 --> 00:18:45,786
We˙ll start the directions hearing
at the digs.

320
00:18:48,660 --> 00:18:52,249
Mrs Hulsey, as the claimant
and the person most inconvenienced

321
00:18:52,690 --> 00:18:55,514
by this action,
I need to ask you some questions.

322
00:18:55,860 --> 00:19:00,110
These you can answer directly yourself
or through your counsel.

323
00:19:00,770 --> 00:19:02,869
- It˙s Ms, sir. I˙m not married.
- I do apologise.

324
00:19:02,870 --> 00:19:05,420
- Is this your son?
- Yes. Michael.

325
00:19:05,840 --> 00:19:09,076
- How old are you, Michael?
- I˙ll be eight on June 5th.

326
00:19:09,210 --> 00:19:12,179
- What˙s the book you˙re reading?
- Lord of the Rings.

327
00:19:12,180 --> 00:19:14,810
Is it? That˙s impressive for seven.

328
00:19:16,080 --> 00:19:18,390
- What book you on?
- Two Towers.

329
00:19:18,820 --> 00:19:22,390
Wowl I was 11
before I read the first book.

330
00:19:22,990 --> 00:19:25,579
- He˙s a very keen reader.
- Yes, he must be.

331
00:19:27,460 --> 00:19:31,910
Ms Hulsey, is it your earnest
intention to pursue this claim?

332
00:19:32,600 --> 00:19:34,270
It is. It˙s why I˙m here.

333
00:19:34,570 --> 00:19:37,500
Well, you see,
it needn˙t necessarily follow.

334
00:19:37,970 --> 00:19:39,739
No doubt your barrister, Mrs Mills,

335
00:19:39,740 --> 00:19:42,809
has explained to you what will happen,
but believe me,

336
00:19:42,810 --> 00:19:45,693
whatever she has said,
that˙s only the half of it.

337
00:19:47,580 --> 00:19:52,090
Is your health such that
you˙re likely to be able to finish?

338
00:19:52,820 --> 00:19:56,409
Some days it˙s better than others.
I˙m determined to continue.

339
00:20:00,290 --> 00:20:03,029
This case was to have been
heard by Mr Justice Everard.

340
00:20:03,030 --> 00:20:04,769
Would you prefer to wait for him

341
00:20:04,770 --> 00:20:07,320
or are you content
to be heard here by me?

342
00:20:11,970 --> 00:20:14,439
My client would prefer to
be heard as soon as possible.

343
00:20:14,440 --> 00:20:18,349
She feels that if there are protracted
delays, her health might give out.

344
00:20:18,350 --> 00:20:21,219
All right. And do you understand
that this is just a hearing

345
00:20:21,220 --> 00:20:23,590
about whether evidence is admissible

346
00:20:24,020 --> 00:20:26,903
and that should
the defendant˙s arguments prevail,

347
00:20:27,050 --> 00:20:28,689
I might prevent you from proceeding?

348
00:20:28,690 --> 00:20:31,970
Yes, Jo...
Mrs Mills explained that to me.

349
00:20:32,490 --> 00:20:36,120
All right. Is there any way
that this case can be settled

350
00:20:36,700 --> 00:20:39,070
without recourse to the court?

351
00:20:39,500 --> 00:20:41,699
We don˙t accept
there is a case to settle.

352
00:20:41,700 --> 00:20:43,810
No need to stand. Mrs Mills?

353
00:20:44,170 --> 00:20:46,969
The defendants have approached us
on several occasions

354
00:20:46,970 --> 00:20:49,239
to argue points they feel
will invalidate the case.

355
00:20:49,240 --> 00:20:51,320
We don˙t accept the contention.

356
00:20:51,680 --> 00:20:54,151
Then it seems likely
you˙re going to trial.

357
00:20:54,950 --> 00:20:57,349
But the case is ready
to go before a judge?

358
00:20:57,350 --> 00:20:59,689
That˙s what we˙re about to hear,
Ms Hulsey. Shall we start?

359
00:20:59,690 --> 00:21:03,500
Before we do, My Lord,
I have a point of law I˙d like to raise.

360
00:21:04,830 --> 00:21:07,289
- Go on.
- I wish to challenge the legality

361
00:21:07,290 --> 00:21:10,585
of your conducting this hearing
in this informal setting.

362
00:21:10,900 --> 00:21:13,499
You have an authority to support
this challenge, Mrs Channing?

363
00:21:13,500 --> 00:21:16,799
Well, I˙m sure I do, My Lord,
if only I were in a formal setting

364
00:21:16,800 --> 00:21:18,939
where I had access to Halsbury˙s Laws.

365
00:21:18,940 --> 00:21:22,690
Well, alas, we are not and you don˙t.

366
00:21:23,310 --> 00:21:24,860
However, I am certain

367
00:21:25,180 --> 00:21:28,649
that my decision to hold
the directions hearing here is sound.

368
00:21:28,650 --> 00:21:31,592
The Supreme Court Act 1981
is a bit like the Bible.

369
00:21:32,850 --> 00:21:36,027
- My Lord, I wish to develop my argument.
- Then do so.

370
00:21:37,190 --> 00:21:40,060
You know very well I can˙t at present.

371
00:21:41,230 --> 00:21:43,499
Then, Mrs Channing,
to quote Wittgenstein,

372
00:21:43,500 --> 00:21:46,795
"Whereof we cannot speak,
thereof we must remain silent."

373
00:21:47,500 --> 00:21:51,570
Let˙s hear your argument on the evidence
you want excluded, shall we?

374
00:21:52,210 --> 00:21:54,539
I cannot remain silent
and witness an injustice

375
00:21:54,540 --> 00:21:56,305
perpetrated against my client.

376
00:21:56,410 --> 00:22:00,379
If you don˙t remain silent on other than
the substance of the pre-trial hearing,

377
00:22:00,380 --> 00:22:03,579
I˙ll hold you in contempt,
and this is your only warning.

378
00:22:03,580 --> 00:22:07,522
Is this going to be your argument
on the evidence you want excluded?

379
00:22:09,590 --> 00:22:13,229
As you will know from your papers,
My Lord, my argument relates

380
00:22:13,230 --> 00:22:16,529
to an email the claimant
illegally obtained from the internet.

381
00:22:16,530 --> 00:22:20,570
I˙d gladly develop my argument,
if only I had access to my papers,

382
00:22:21,200 --> 00:22:25,740
which at this moment sit
inaccessible in Number 2 court.

383
00:22:28,040 --> 00:22:30,511
Fine. Well, then,
having started this case,

384
00:22:30,610 --> 00:22:32,493
I will adjourn it till tomorrow.

385
00:22:32,580 --> 00:22:35,219
When, Mr Clerk, I˙m afraid
I˙m unable to say at present.

386
00:22:35,220 --> 00:22:36,926
But I would like both parties

387
00:22:37,050 --> 00:22:39,489
to stand by until
I˙m free of my other case.

388
00:22:39,490 --> 00:22:41,220
Until the morning.

389
00:22:42,660 --> 00:22:43,680
Jo?

390
00:22:46,360 --> 00:22:50,360
I˙m afraid the Disciplinary Tribunal
is going ahead with the hearing.

391
00:22:52,530 --> 00:22:54,939
They keep me waiting on
tenterhooks for 11 months

392
00:22:54,940 --> 00:22:56,939
and I get to hear it from you. Great.

393
00:22:56,940 --> 00:23:00,109
I meant what I promised, Jo.
I will fight them with you.

394
00:23:00,110 --> 00:23:02,309
My head of chambers thinks
that might make matters worse.

395
00:23:02,310 --> 00:23:03,579
Do we know who˙s chairing the hearing?

396
00:23:03,580 --> 00:23:06,249
No, it˙ll be a High Court judge, though.
You˙ll come out of this all right.

397
00:23:06,250 --> 00:23:08,949
I don˙t want to come out all right,
I want to be cleared.

398
00:23:08,950 --> 00:23:10,590
It˙s all your fault anyway.

399
00:23:21,060 --> 00:23:24,690
- They didn˙t find any bombs, John.
- No, I bet they didn˙t.

400
00:23:28,140 --> 00:23:31,169
Now, I find it hard to believe
that a senior civil servant

401
00:23:31,170 --> 00:23:33,839
would pull such a stroke
just to stop you hearing a case.

402
00:23:33,840 --> 00:23:36,609
Oh, I doubt if Ian
actually made the call himself.

403
00:23:36,610 --> 00:23:38,570
Or the circuit administrator.

404
00:23:40,010 --> 00:23:42,349
But if it was done
and they˙ve involved someone else

405
00:23:42,350 --> 00:23:44,579
they˙re at an even greater risk
of exposure.

406
00:23:44,580 --> 00:23:47,330
You have to consider what˙s at stake.

407
00:23:48,390 --> 00:23:52,200
Mobile phone licences are worth
￡22 billion to the government.

408
00:23:52,830 --> 00:23:54,959
If juries start finding
in favour of claimants,

409
00:23:54,960 --> 00:23:58,059
they could blow some of these mobile
phone companies right out of the water.

410
00:23:58,060 --> 00:24:00,569
Presumably their insurance companies
would pay.

411
00:24:00,570 --> 00:24:03,530
Insurance companies
are now declining cover.

412
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:05,765
Yes, well, I see the pressure.

413
00:24:05,840 --> 00:24:08,193
I˙ll try to find out
more about the bomb.

414
00:24:09,380 --> 00:24:10,960
A word, sir?

415
00:24:15,220 --> 00:24:17,360
Do you want your clerk to hear this?

416
00:24:18,920 --> 00:24:20,919
Well, I can˙t think
it˙s anything that she won˙t know

417
00:24:20,920 --> 00:24:24,097
within five minutes of your
leaving my chambers, Monty.

418
00:24:24,390 --> 00:24:26,920
This is going to be
a difficult term for us.

419
00:24:28,230 --> 00:24:31,040
- Is there a problem?
- Oh, don˙t be cute, sir.

420
00:24:31,500 --> 00:24:34,369
I˙m the Presider. I have
seniority in court and in lodgings.

421
00:24:34,370 --> 00:24:36,680
You took my room at the digs.

422
00:24:37,100 --> 00:24:40,239
Well, I˙ll move my things
if it matters so much to you.

423
00:24:40,240 --> 00:24:44,110
The issue is one of good manners.
I˙ve moved to a hotel.

424
00:24:44,740 --> 00:24:46,949
What is of greater
importance is the case

425
00:24:46,950 --> 00:24:48,779
that you˙ve taken which I was hearing.

426
00:24:48,780 --> 00:24:52,016
No, I take cases as they arrive,
the cab-rank principle.

427
00:24:52,490 --> 00:24:56,961
You˙d declined to sit, so as the next
most senior judge, the case came to me.

428
00:24:57,090 --> 00:24:58,729
No, I was going to
hear it in the Strand

429
00:24:58,730 --> 00:25:01,829
but the claimant was ill,
so I agreed to hear it on circuit.

430
00:25:01,830 --> 00:25:02,929
Well, if you wanted the case

431
00:25:02,930 --> 00:25:05,699
the normal procedure
is to reserve it to yourself.

432
00:25:05,700 --> 00:25:09,112
Don˙t presume to instruct me
in procedure, I know the form.

433
00:25:09,470 --> 00:25:10,647
I want my case back.

434
00:25:10,870 --> 00:25:15,223
I˙m happy to give you the room, Monty,
but the negligence case is now mine.

435
00:25:15,710 --> 00:25:19,710
No, sir, you will observe the correct
procedure and give the case up.

436
00:25:19,810 --> 00:25:21,210
No.

437
00:25:21,480 --> 00:25:25,598
In all my days on the bench, I have
never encountered such discourtesy.

438
00:25:26,050 --> 00:25:28,992
I˙m shocked by your behaviour.
I won˙t tolerate it.

439
00:25:29,060 --> 00:25:32,002
You˙ll come to regret
crossing swords with me, sir.

440
00:25:32,160 --> 00:25:34,330
All right. Still don˙t get the case.

441
00:25:34,690 --> 00:25:38,279
I seriously doubt your ability
to hear this or any other case.

442
00:25:40,970 --> 00:25:42,170
Goodbye, Monty.

443
00:25:49,410 --> 00:25:52,149
Oh, dear. Hadn˙t you better
let him have the case, Judge?

444
00:25:52,150 --> 00:25:54,020
He is the Presider, after all.

445
00:25:55,020 --> 00:25:58,256
In another country
one might suspect him of being venal.

446
00:25:58,990 --> 00:26:01,814
He could have shares
in the mobile phone company.

447
00:26:02,220 --> 00:26:04,689
Oh, I doubt it.
He˙d have to recuse himself.

448
00:26:04,690 --> 00:26:07,689
You know what they call him
around the Strand, Judge?

449
00:26:07,690 --> 00:26:09,970
You mustn˙t repeat such things.

450
00:26:11,430 --> 00:26:13,980
Yes, I know. Legover Everard.

451
00:26:18,970 --> 00:26:21,400
Keep your ear to the ground, Coop.

452
00:26:21,810 --> 00:26:24,928
This won˙t be their last attempt
to get the case back.

453
00:26:29,820 --> 00:26:32,762
You˙re up about late, Ian.
Or did you dine locally?

454
00:26:32,990 --> 00:26:34,860
I came bearing an olive branch.

455
00:26:35,220 --> 00:26:36,890
Oh, do we need one?

456
00:26:46,370 --> 00:26:50,429
We cannot allow this rift to deepen
between you and Sir Monty Everard.

457
00:26:51,710 --> 00:26:54,290
I agreed to give him the best room.

458
00:26:54,740 --> 00:26:58,211
What I won˙t agree is to relinquish
a case I˙ve now started.

459
00:26:58,510 --> 00:27:00,628
I understand the principle entirely.

460
00:27:00,880 --> 00:27:02,810
Does Monty Everard?

461
00:27:03,180 --> 00:27:07,400
This is so unnecessary.
It seems Mrs Channing is to offer you

462
00:27:08,050 --> 00:27:12,050
an argument that will provide you
with a neat exit from this impasse.

463
00:27:13,760 --> 00:27:15,510
Is it an impasse?

464
00:27:17,160 --> 00:27:18,500
Oh, thank you.

465
00:27:24,300 --> 00:27:26,990
This is entirely coincidental, John.

466
00:27:28,240 --> 00:27:32,080
But there is a vacancy arising
on the Appellate Bench.

467
00:27:34,180 --> 00:27:37,020
Regardless of the opposition
from your brethren,

468
00:27:38,350 --> 00:27:41,056
the Lord Chancellor
is keen for you to fill it.

469
00:27:43,520 --> 00:27:45,980
Are you making me an offer?

470
00:27:46,430 --> 00:27:49,019
You know we only have
the power to recommend.

471
00:27:49,130 --> 00:27:52,169
The Queen would appoint you,
should you indicate your willingness

472
00:27:52,170 --> 00:27:54,310
to accept the elevation.

473
00:27:55,800 --> 00:27:56,800
Well,

474
00:27:58,970 --> 00:28:01,400
in view of our past, Ian,

475
00:28:01,810 --> 00:28:04,340
I think I would need
to see the small print.

476
00:28:06,780 --> 00:28:10,650
Judge, Mrs Mills has just heard
the Disciplinary Tribunal

477
00:28:11,250 --> 00:28:12,780
is on Thursday and Friday.

478
00:28:12,920 --> 00:28:16,080
This Thursday and Friday?
What brought that on?

479
00:28:16,620 --> 00:28:18,189
Did she say who˙s chairing it?

480
00:28:18,190 --> 00:28:19,829
Only that she needs to be free of court.

481
00:28:19,830 --> 00:28:23,129
She doesn˙t want to leave the beginning
of the mobile phone case to her junior.

482
00:28:23,130 --> 00:28:26,542
That case might not even run, Coop,
at least not before me.

483
00:28:33,310 --> 00:28:35,339
I trust you now have some authority

484
00:28:35,340 --> 00:28:38,180
for your argument yesterday,
Mrs Channing?

485
00:28:38,650 --> 00:28:43,510
St Edmonsbury and Ipswich
Diocesan Board of Finance and Clarke,

486
00:28:44,250 --> 00:28:47,470
Chancery cases 1973, at page 323,

487
00:28:48,020 --> 00:28:50,740
was what I was groping for, My Lord.

488
00:28:53,560 --> 00:28:56,960
Mr Justice McGarry
took the Chancery Division to Suffolk

489
00:28:57,500 --> 00:29:00,971
to take evidence from
an 84-year-old witness in poor health.

490
00:29:01,130 --> 00:29:04,542
He did so without the authorisation
of the Lord Chancellor.

491
00:29:04,970 --> 00:29:08,539
The Appeal Court judge held
that it wasn˙t constitutional to do so

492
00:29:08,540 --> 00:29:10,470
and overturned the judgement.

493
00:29:11,980 --> 00:29:15,730
I confess to not being familiar
with this case, Mrs Channing.

494
00:29:17,780 --> 00:29:20,219
Accepting that time
is against the claimant,

495
00:29:20,220 --> 00:29:22,868
I could overrule you
for compassionate reasons

496
00:29:22,920 --> 00:29:24,789
as I have heard nothing
so far to suggest

497
00:29:24,790 --> 00:29:28,160
that anything I did
is remotely anomalous in law.

498
00:29:28,700 --> 00:29:30,430
I am reluctant to do so.

499
00:29:30,730 --> 00:29:32,429
If anyjudgement
in favour of the claimant

500
00:29:32,430 --> 00:29:34,830
might be overruled on a technicality.

501
00:29:35,270 --> 00:29:37,599
Although as you are
perfectly well aware,

502
00:29:37,600 --> 00:29:40,309
I made no ruling
other than to adjourn the hearing.

503
00:29:40,310 --> 00:29:44,310
I see no point in proceeding with this
until I˙ve read the authority.

504
00:29:44,540 --> 00:29:46,179
So, I˙ll give my ruling
first thing in the morning.

505
00:29:46,180 --> 00:29:47,180
My Lord...

506
00:29:47,380 --> 00:29:48,979
I˙ll hear from you first thing,
Mrs Mills. Till morning.

507
00:29:48,980 --> 00:29:50,410
USHER: All rise.

508
00:29:52,990 --> 00:29:54,330
Are we winning?

509
00:29:57,720 --> 00:29:59,589
The fitness to plead
in the causing death

510
00:29:59,590 --> 00:30:02,120
by dangerous driving case
is waiting, Judge.

511
00:30:02,130 --> 00:30:04,530
Is the defendant present now?

512
00:30:04,930 --> 00:30:07,510
Yes, Judge,
with an army of psychiatrists.

513
00:30:07,930 --> 00:30:10,740
Oh, I˙m sure Mr Brooklands
can afford them.

514
00:30:13,310 --> 00:30:15,509
Perhaps you˙d get me some coffee, Coop?

515
00:30:15,510 --> 00:30:16,510
Yeah.

516
00:30:19,110 --> 00:30:20,450
(MOBILE PHONE RINGING)

517
00:30:22,080 --> 00:30:24,316
- Are you still in the building?
- Yes.

518
00:30:24,450 --> 00:30:25,820
Fancy some coffee?

519
00:30:26,090 --> 00:30:28,119
I don˙t think so,
not without George present.

520
00:30:28,120 --> 00:30:30,062
Oh, we won˙t talk about the case.

521
00:30:30,720 --> 00:30:32,010
Anyway, Coop's here.

522
00:30:32,430 --> 00:30:35,029
We can invite George
if it˙d make you feel more comfortable.

523
00:30:35,030 --> 00:30:38,619
She already embroiders this relationship
more than she should.

524
00:30:38,930 --> 00:30:40,329
Thanks, Coop. Would you mind staying?

525
00:30:40,330 --> 00:30:43,169
We are going to discuss
Mrs Mills˙ disciplinary hearing.

526
00:30:43,170 --> 00:30:46,569
Well, I˙ve been called as a witness,
Judge, for the Bar Council.

527
00:30:46,570 --> 00:30:49,139
Ah, I forgot.
You˙ve been asked to give a statement.

528
00:30:49,140 --> 00:30:51,376
Well, no, perhaps you better not stay.

529
00:30:52,610 --> 00:30:54,679
I don˙t know what they think
I can tell them.

530
00:30:54,680 --> 00:30:58,504
What you can or cannot tell them
is not what interests them, Coop.

531
00:31:04,490 --> 00:31:06,785
They really mean to get me, don˙t they?

532
00:31:10,160 --> 00:31:11,620
(GAVEL POUNDING)

533
00:31:11,900 --> 00:31:13,300
USHER: All rise.

534
00:31:22,540 --> 00:31:25,305
My apologies for the delay.
I have another case.

535
00:31:26,550 --> 00:31:29,492
There is difficulty in being
in two places at once.

536
00:31:29,820 --> 00:31:31,520
Now then, Mr Petros.

537
00:31:34,050 --> 00:31:36,403
Does your client understand where he is?

538
00:31:36,490 --> 00:31:39,480
More importantly,
does he know why he˙s here?

539
00:31:39,990 --> 00:31:41,980
Alas, the nature of his illness

540
00:31:42,330 --> 00:31:45,790
is such that he dips in and out
of his cognitive functions.

541
00:31:48,000 --> 00:31:51,980
I would direct you
to the Crown against Pritchard in 1836.

542
00:31:52,610 --> 00:31:54,109
As I am sure Your Lordship is aware,

543
00:31:54,110 --> 00:31:56,522
this is the leading case
of determination.

544
00:31:57,180 --> 00:32:00,769
Baron Allison characterised
this most important question thus,

545
00:32:02,250 --> 00:32:04,603
"Is the defendant
of sufficient intellect

546
00:32:04,680 --> 00:32:07,916
"to comprehend the course
of the proceedings in a trial,

547
00:32:08,020 --> 00:32:10,130
"so as to make a proper defence?"

548
00:32:11,790 --> 00:32:13,659
The answer here, you will discover,

549
00:32:13,660 --> 00:32:16,970
on examining both
the defendant and the doctors

550
00:32:18,300 --> 00:32:19,769
is certainly that he is not.

551
00:32:19,770 --> 00:32:23,476
Yes, thank you, Mr Petros,
I am familiar with this leading case.

552
00:32:23,600 --> 00:32:25,350
In current law,

553
00:32:25,710 --> 00:32:28,240
the question to be tried
by a judge and jury

554
00:32:28,310 --> 00:32:32,663
is whether the defendant is capable
of giving, receiving, and understanding

555
00:32:33,050 --> 00:32:35,379
communications relating
to a criminal trial.

556
00:32:35,380 --> 00:32:39,130
The issues the court
will focus on in deciding are

557
00:32:39,750 --> 00:32:42,970
the challenging of a jury,
the idea of a plea,

558
00:32:43,490 --> 00:32:45,800
evidence given in the trial and

559
00:32:46,230 --> 00:32:49,230
whether he is capable
of instructing his solicitors.

560
00:32:49,960 --> 00:32:52,078
I assume he did instruct solicitors?

561
00:32:52,430 --> 00:32:55,469
Initially, then, subsequently
he dropped out of the box.

562
00:32:55,470 --> 00:32:57,769
His wife has been
instructing them since.

563
00:32:57,770 --> 00:33:00,006
- Is Mrs Brooklands in court?
- She is.

564
00:33:01,910 --> 00:33:03,720
DEED: Ah, Mrs Brooklands.

565
00:33:04,040 --> 00:33:07,470
Is your husband
capable of giving you his instructions?

566
00:33:08,950 --> 00:33:10,068
No, not any longer.

567
00:33:10,220 --> 00:33:14,103
I instruct his solicitor according
to what I believe he would want.

568
00:33:14,650 --> 00:33:16,110
I see. Thank you.

569
00:33:23,660 --> 00:33:26,778
You see, the crime with which
Mr Brooklands is charged

570
00:33:28,400 --> 00:33:30,240
is extraordinarily grave.

571
00:33:32,170 --> 00:33:36,406
That he recklessly drove his car
into two young children and their mother

572
00:33:36,840 --> 00:33:40,320
causing their death
and then wantonly left the scene.

573
00:33:40,880 --> 00:33:43,980
Were he to walk away from court
without a trial,

574
00:33:44,480 --> 00:33:47,580
either to establish his mental condition

575
00:33:48,090 --> 00:33:51,960
or to see if he was indeed the author
of this dreadful tragedy,

576
00:33:52,590 --> 00:33:54,414
might lead the public to wonder

577
00:33:54,430 --> 00:33:56,842
whether justice is possible
under the law.

578
00:33:57,560 --> 00:34:01,149
We must first establish
whether the defendant is fit to plead.

579
00:34:01,670 --> 00:34:04,200
Sp let us empanel a jury
for the first part.

580
00:34:04,370 --> 00:34:06,739
My Lord, would you hear
an argument in chambers?

581
00:34:06,740 --> 00:34:07,940
No, Mr Petros,

582
00:34:08,170 --> 00:34:11,935
this is a matter now for a jury,
for the reasons I˙ve just given.

583
00:34:12,580 --> 00:34:15,070
Let˙s have the jury candidates,
Mr Clerk.

584
00:34:16,780 --> 00:34:20,289
I˙m not sure, but I think I might
agree with defence counsel.

585
00:34:20,290 --> 00:34:23,319
There˙s a possibility Brooklands
might not get a fair trial.

586
00:34:23,320 --> 00:34:25,220
You must be joking, Judge.

587
00:34:25,590 --> 00:34:27,659
Do you think he˙s faking his illness?

588
00:34:27,660 --> 00:34:29,859
You can˙t let him avoid
his responsibilities.

589
00:34:29,860 --> 00:34:31,850
Men do that too easily, Judge.

590
00:34:43,110 --> 00:34:45,040
We going to school or court?

591
00:34:47,850 --> 00:34:48,850
Mum?

592
00:35:03,100 --> 00:35:05,748
I˙m sorry you have to
cope with this, Michael.

593
00:35:06,170 --> 00:35:08,420
You˙re so very brave.

594
00:35:08,800 --> 00:35:10,939
Most adults wouldn˙t
have a clue what to do.

595
00:35:10,940 --> 00:35:12,690
Have you got to stay here?

596
00:35:13,010 --> 00:35:14,716
No, I won˙t do that, darling.

597
00:35:14,810 --> 00:35:16,560
- Hi, Michael.
- Hi, Armac.

598
00:35:19,780 --> 00:35:21,957
What are we going to do with you, Di?

599
00:35:24,880 --> 00:35:26,520
Has it got worse?

600
00:35:26,820 --> 00:35:29,480
It˙s now a grade 4 astrocytoma.

601
00:35:29,920 --> 00:35:31,685
They don˙t get any better, Di.

602
00:35:33,230 --> 00:35:36,407
Did you think any more
about that radical radiotherapy?

603
00:35:38,160 --> 00:35:39,620
Have the odds improved?

604
00:35:41,470 --> 00:35:42,930
It˙s still six-to-one.

605
00:35:43,200 --> 00:35:44,840
But if we leave it, then...

606
00:35:48,170 --> 00:35:51,759
- How˙s your case going?
- Well, at least it˙s going to court.

607
00:35:53,580 --> 00:35:55,522
Mmm. You˙re not going to give up.

608
00:35:57,950 --> 00:36:02,110
Well, here we are again. All very cosy.

609
00:36:02,790 --> 00:36:04,989
John just itching to give you
your way again.

610
00:36:04,990 --> 00:36:08,520
George, what is your problem?
Are you still in love with him?

611
00:36:09,230 --> 00:36:10,701
I don˙t think I ever was.

612
00:36:10,860 --> 00:36:12,499
Then why don˙t you leave him alone?

613
00:36:12,500 --> 00:36:15,500
It˙s the unfairness
of the situation for my clients.

614
00:36:15,540 --> 00:36:18,499
How could you be married to
a man for so long, bear his child,

615
00:36:18,500 --> 00:36:20,269
and still know so little about him?

616
00:36:20,270 --> 00:36:22,918
John would never say
or do anything dishonest.

617
00:36:23,310 --> 00:36:26,650
- There˙s no denying you were in his bed.
- When?

618
00:36:27,180 --> 00:36:29,080
I˙ve seen the photos.

619
00:36:29,420 --> 00:36:30,790
Photos, what photos?

620
00:36:31,050 --> 00:36:35,049
I˙d like to know how he˙s going to save
this career he˙s worked so hard on.

621
00:36:35,050 --> 00:36:36,689
George, what is that meant to mean?

622
00:36:36,690 --> 00:36:38,270
You work it out.

623
00:36:39,260 --> 00:36:40,690
They˙re ready in court.

624
00:36:42,300 --> 00:36:44,329
- What photos? Who took them?
- You tell me.

625
00:36:44,330 --> 00:36:46,869
They˙re your rooms I was in.
I was drunk, remember?

626
00:36:46,870 --> 00:36:48,459
A spy sneaked into my room.

627
00:36:48,500 --> 00:36:51,669
You know how much faith courts
and tribunals place in such evidence.

628
00:36:51,670 --> 00:36:52,670
I˙ll be finished.

629
00:36:52,770 --> 00:36:54,000
(KNOCK ON DOOR)

630
00:36:55,780 --> 00:36:58,770
Mrs Channing,
I have read your authority,

631
00:36:59,250 --> 00:37:01,949
and it seems to me that you˙ve
completely reversed the meaning.

632
00:37:01,950 --> 00:37:04,656
Either that
or you˙re being entirely facetious.

633
00:37:04,850 --> 00:37:06,189
Oh, entirely facetious.

634
00:37:06,190 --> 00:37:08,710
Mrs Channing, you˙ll have to speak up.

635
00:37:09,120 --> 00:37:10,532
I do apologise, My Lord,

636
00:37:10,720 --> 00:37:14,250
in my haste to prevent an injustice,
I misread the authority.

637
00:37:14,290 --> 00:37:17,029
However, there is another
point of law I˙d like to raise

638
00:37:17,030 --> 00:37:19,469
concerning your hearing
this case, My Lord.

639
00:37:19,470 --> 00:37:21,353
- You surprise me.
- Doesn˙t she?

640
00:37:23,470 --> 00:37:27,353
With respect, My Lord, my argument
concerns your hearing this case.

641
00:37:29,110 --> 00:37:33,060
In view of your personal involvement
with counsel for the claimant,

642
00:37:33,710 --> 00:37:37,240
my client fails to see
how they can get an impartial hearing.

643
00:37:37,250 --> 00:37:39,192
That would be a matter for appeal

644
00:37:39,220 --> 00:37:41,691
should this court
find against your client.

645
00:37:41,820 --> 00:37:44,589
Well, it˙s a simple matter, My Lord,
that can be resolved here.

646
00:37:44,590 --> 00:37:46,629
My clients would feel more comfortable

647
00:37:46,630 --> 00:37:49,099
were you to recuse yourself
from this case.

648
00:37:49,100 --> 00:37:52,806
The functioning of justice
in this country is entirely dependent

649
00:37:52,930 --> 00:37:55,680
upon the impartiality of the judiciary.

650
00:37:56,140 --> 00:37:58,169
As Lord Bingham pointed out in Locabail,

651
00:37:58,170 --> 00:38:01,860
All England Reports 2000,
Volume 1, page 69,

652
00:38:02,440 --> 00:38:05,709
that right is guaranteed by the European
Convention of Human and Rights.

653
00:38:05,710 --> 00:38:08,260
Do you want the full quotation? No.

654
00:38:09,650 --> 00:38:12,889
GEORGE: I would direct Your Lordship
to the Pinochet Appeal 2000.

655
00:38:12,890 --> 00:38:15,049
The House of Lords held that
the appeal was unsafe

656
00:38:15,050 --> 00:38:16,889
because the wife
of one of the appealjudges

657
00:38:16,890 --> 00:38:19,189
had a relationship
with Amnesty International.

658
00:38:19,190 --> 00:38:20,838
I fail to see the relevance.

659
00:38:20,890 --> 00:38:22,859
There the connection,
if tenuous, was plain.

660
00:38:22,860 --> 00:38:24,859
I have no relationship with Mrs Mills,

661
00:38:24,860 --> 00:38:28,069
other than the fact that like many other
barristers, she appears before me.

662
00:38:28,070 --> 00:38:30,620
My Lord, this won˙t satisfy my clients.

663
00:38:31,070 --> 00:38:34,541
Mrs Channing, judges are not there
to be chosen by litigants

664
00:38:34,940 --> 00:38:36,529
as they might choose a car.

665
00:38:36,640 --> 00:38:40,464
She˙s appearing before a Disciplinary
Tribunal of the Bar Council.

666
00:38:40,610 --> 00:38:41,809
DEED: No, no, no. No, that˙s enough.

667
00:38:41,810 --> 00:38:43,749
GEORGE: I want to address you
on what she˙s accused of doing.

668
00:38:43,750 --> 00:38:46,149
No. I have ruled.
This matter is now closed.

669
00:38:46,150 --> 00:38:49,119
It˙s something for the Court of Appeal
if this goes against your clients.

670
00:38:49,120 --> 00:38:51,473
Now, do you have any more points of law?

671
00:38:51,860 --> 00:38:55,390
Might we have a short adjournment
while I consult my clients?

672
00:38:57,030 --> 00:38:58,490
Fifteen minutes?

673
00:39:02,170 --> 00:39:05,399
Ian, I˙ve just been called out of
a cabinet sub-committee

674
00:39:05,400 --> 00:39:06,740
by George Channing.

675
00:39:08,810 --> 00:39:11,770
- You know what I˙m about to say.
- Minister.

676
00:39:13,850 --> 00:39:17,203
We˙re doing all we can to wrestle
the case away from Deed.

677
00:39:17,250 --> 00:39:20,349
That˙s easy in theory,
in practice it proves very difficult.

678
00:39:20,350 --> 00:39:22,292
This is getting out of hand, Ian.

679
00:39:22,520 --> 00:39:25,289
All the ministers with an interest
are unanimous on this.

680
00:39:25,290 --> 00:39:27,340
Then perhaps they have a solution.

681
00:39:27,690 --> 00:39:30,650
No. That is for our civil servants
to come up with.

682
00:39:32,160 --> 00:39:35,269
I˙m in the hot seat because
the Department of Trade and Industry

683
00:39:35,270 --> 00:39:37,110
sold these cell phone licences.

684
00:39:37,470 --> 00:39:40,739
And, of course, I do find myself
in a rather delicate position

685
00:39:40,740 --> 00:39:42,979
because George Channing
is involved in the case.

686
00:39:42,980 --> 00:39:45,569
Perhaps you could get her
to assassinate him.

687
00:39:46,810 --> 00:39:48,458
We˙d happily supply the gun.

688
00:39:49,480 --> 00:39:51,730
You do realise what˙s at stake here?

689
00:39:55,350 --> 00:39:57,586
Mobile phones are not a health hazard.

690
00:39:57,990 --> 00:40:00,638
Then we˙ve nothing
to concern ourselves about.

691
00:40:00,830 --> 00:40:03,478
Everyone suffers
from such negative publicity.

692
00:40:03,530 --> 00:40:07,069
I doubt it will cost the government
or companies much in lost revenue.

693
00:40:07,070 --> 00:40:10,899
After all, negative publicity hasn˙t
cost the tobacco companies anything.

694
00:40:10,900 --> 00:40:13,077
Mobile phones are entirely different.

695
00:40:13,970 --> 00:40:18,029
Tobacco is not an essential element
of business or social interaction.

696
00:40:18,810 --> 00:40:21,516
If this goes wrong, Ian,
mobile phone companies

697
00:40:21,850 --> 00:40:25,510
could be faced with swingeing
compensation claims against them.

698
00:40:26,090 --> 00:40:28,267
We do have one more lever under Deed,

699
00:40:28,590 --> 00:40:31,767
when Mrs Mills appears
before the disciplinary hearing.

700
00:40:32,660 --> 00:40:33,660
A big shock.

701
00:40:33,860 --> 00:40:36,320
Mmm. It had better amount to something.

702
00:40:43,870 --> 00:40:45,300
(MOBILE PHONE RINGING)

703
00:40:49,440 --> 00:40:50,930
Call me on my land line.

704
00:40:51,440 --> 00:40:52,735
It is my clients˙ wish

705
00:40:52,780 --> 00:40:55,549
that I do not pursue the matter
of your impartiality further, My Lord.

706
00:40:55,550 --> 00:40:56,649
Then can we get on?

707
00:40:56,650 --> 00:40:58,886
For fear it will prejudice their case.

708
00:40:59,220 --> 00:41:01,800
It couldn˙t,
as you well know, Mrs Channing.

709
00:41:03,190 --> 00:41:07,543
Now, you have some objection to the
evidence being offered by the claimant.

710
00:41:08,790 --> 00:41:11,899
The claimant and her counsel
have not only behaved unfairly,

711
00:41:11,900 --> 00:41:14,560
indeed, they have behaved
unlawfully, My Lord.

712
00:41:15,030 --> 00:41:17,869
They have in their possession
copies of erased emails

713
00:41:17,870 --> 00:41:20,047
sent by my clients and to my clients.

714
00:41:20,610 --> 00:41:24,199
These they obtained in contravention
of the laws of procedure.

715
00:41:24,440 --> 00:41:26,809
Were these documents
sought in disclosure, Mrs Mills?

716
00:41:26,810 --> 00:41:29,458
They were, My Lord.
Despite frequent requests,

717
00:41:29,880 --> 00:41:32,049
the defendants failed to produce them.

718
00:41:32,050 --> 00:41:35,419
For the simple reason they did not
exist as disclosable documents

719
00:41:35,420 --> 00:41:37,189
at the time this action was started.

720
00:41:37,190 --> 00:41:41,367
Mrs Channing, documents, whether
physical or electronic, are disclosable

721
00:41:41,690 --> 00:41:42,859
if they relate to the case.

722
00:41:42,860 --> 00:41:44,829
These relate directly to the case.

723
00:41:44,830 --> 00:41:47,830
The documents contain a report
showing a causal link

724
00:41:48,070 --> 00:41:52,188
between the radiation in the form
of microwaves produced by cell phones

725
00:41:52,270 --> 00:41:55,810
and scrambled patterns
of electrical activity in the brain.

726
00:41:56,380 --> 00:41:58,379
This leads to the stripping
of proteins from cells

727
00:41:58,380 --> 00:42:00,049
which can in turn lead to tumours.

728
00:42:00,050 --> 00:42:02,879
These do sound like disclosable
documents, Mrs Channing.

729
00:42:02,880 --> 00:42:05,528
Had they existed
in the control of my clients.

730
00:42:05,680 --> 00:42:08,849
The fact is they had erased them
long before this action was started.

731
00:42:08,850 --> 00:42:10,919
So our contention is that
they are not disclosable

732
00:42:10,920 --> 00:42:12,970
and should not be admissible.

733
00:42:14,690 --> 00:42:16,749
Well, it˙s a fair point, Mrs Mills.

734
00:42:16,960 --> 00:42:20,369
Are we to assume that these documents
form the main plank of your case?

735
00:42:20,370 --> 00:42:24,782
They are important supportive evidence
and show the defendant˙s culpability.

736
00:42:25,000 --> 00:42:28,295
One-Way was alerted to the problems,
the health problems,

737
00:42:28,340 --> 00:42:29,600
their product posed.

738
00:42:29,840 --> 00:42:34,060
Now, instead of accepting the advice
of independent scientists,

739
00:42:34,750 --> 00:42:38,620
they chose to erase
both the paper and electronic trails.

740
00:42:39,220 --> 00:42:42,103
We now have a situation
where there is a floodgate

741
00:42:42,250 --> 00:42:44,250
waiting to burst open with claims,

742
00:42:44,590 --> 00:42:47,340
and a company,
whose sole economic imperative

743
00:42:47,830 --> 00:42:50,050
is to keep the gates firmly closed.

744
00:42:51,700 --> 00:42:55,270
My Lord, my clients
are an internationally quoted company

745
00:42:55,870 --> 00:42:58,669
with a reputation both
in employment and social equity

746
00:42:58,670 --> 00:43:00,340
which they guard jealously.

747
00:43:00,670 --> 00:43:04,906
There is no question of their attempting
to foist a deceit on the public.

748
00:43:05,110 --> 00:43:08,009
To do so would be counterproductive
to their business

749
00:43:08,010 --> 00:43:10,090
and run counter to their ethos.

750
00:43:11,180 --> 00:43:14,430
Profit does not equate
with social irresponsibility,

751
00:43:14,990 --> 00:43:17,259
as my learned friend
seems to be suggesting.

752
00:43:17,260 --> 00:43:19,731
The question a jury
might reasonably ask is

753
00:43:20,130 --> 00:43:23,060
why did the company erase the emails?

754
00:43:24,500 --> 00:43:28,971
The reason is simply that if my clients
had to keep every electronic message,

755
00:43:29,470 --> 00:43:33,009
they would be required to maintain
unimaginable hordes of documents

756
00:43:33,010 --> 00:43:36,050
in an electronic warehouse
in cyberspace.

757
00:43:37,440 --> 00:43:39,509
It˙s not an unreasonable
argument, Mrs Mills.

758
00:43:39,510 --> 00:43:43,049
It wouldn˙t be unreasonable until
you consider the content of the emails

759
00:43:43,050 --> 00:43:44,403
and who they were from.

760
00:43:44,580 --> 00:43:48,345
Independent scientists who One-Way
employed to test their phones.

761
00:43:48,490 --> 00:43:51,373
Correspondence from whom
they were happy to retain

762
00:43:51,820 --> 00:43:54,370
until they began receiving
negative findings

763
00:43:54,830 --> 00:43:57,429
which no longer supported their
contention that their phones were safe.

764
00:43:57,430 --> 00:43:59,629
If you look in your bundle marked A,
My Lord,

765
00:43:59,630 --> 00:44:01,820
you will see a trail of emails

766
00:44:02,230 --> 00:44:04,939
and supporting correspondence
from the early development

767
00:44:04,940 --> 00:44:07,900
of the ZP-9 phone,
the type Ms Hulsey used.

768
00:44:08,370 --> 00:44:11,120
On average there were some
11 emails a day

769
00:44:11,580 --> 00:44:13,779
passing between the company
and the scientists,

770
00:44:13,780 --> 00:44:16,440
up until December 3, 2000,

771
00:44:16,880 --> 00:44:19,600
when they announced
their negative findings.

772
00:44:20,050 --> 00:44:22,950
The gap between December 4th
and December 12th

773
00:44:23,420 --> 00:44:27,185
are the crucial missing emails
which spelt out the causal linkage

774
00:44:27,690 --> 00:44:30,560
between the phones
and the damaged brain cells.

775
00:44:31,060 --> 00:44:34,570
On the 13th of December,
the emails resumed,

776
00:44:35,130 --> 00:44:37,000
terminating their contract.

777
00:44:38,540 --> 00:44:40,909
You have a logical explanation,
Mrs Channing?

778
00:44:40,910 --> 00:44:44,381
I˙m reliably informed by the company
that during that period

779
00:44:44,410 --> 00:44:46,940
they were being attacked
by internet viruses

780
00:44:47,380 --> 00:44:50,570
and that their network was shut down
for long periods.

781
00:44:51,350 --> 00:44:54,880
Then how do you account for these emails
that were retrieved?

782
00:44:55,150 --> 00:44:59,050
We would argue there is no proof
they are our clients˙ documents.

783
00:44:59,660 --> 00:45:01,180
Yeah, all right,

784
00:45:01,460 --> 00:45:05,029
in which case you are right
to stoutly resist their inclusion.

785
00:45:05,030 --> 00:45:07,080
I think the safest way to proceed

786
00:45:07,470 --> 00:45:10,099
would be to call the expert
who retrieved these documents

787
00:45:10,100 --> 00:45:12,806
and find out whether or not
they are forgeries.

788
00:45:13,270 --> 00:45:16,020
Do I have the written report
of this expert?

789
00:45:16,510 --> 00:45:19,030
Its in your bundle, marked 6A, My Lord.

790
00:45:23,220 --> 00:45:25,360
Yes. Okay. Got it.

791
00:45:25,750 --> 00:45:27,456
And is the witness available?

792
00:45:30,290 --> 00:45:32,780
- He is, My Lord.
- My Lord,

793
00:45:33,230 --> 00:45:36,690
the admissibility or inadmissibility
of these documents

794
00:45:37,260 --> 00:45:40,672
does not depend on how some
spotty cybernerd retrieved them

795
00:45:40,870 --> 00:45:43,040
but whether they are disclosable.

796
00:45:43,440 --> 00:45:46,100
They were not
because they did not exist.

797
00:45:47,310 --> 00:45:51,210
Only the spotty cybernerd
can tell us that, Mrs Channing.

798
00:45:51,810 --> 00:45:53,222
We˙d better have him in.

799
00:45:55,380 --> 00:45:59,145
Mr Arditti, could you look at the report
marked 6A in your bundle

800
00:45:59,690 --> 00:46:01,090
and tell us what it is?

801
00:46:02,690 --> 00:46:06,530
A report I compiled based on
recovered material to One-Way PLC.

802
00:46:07,130 --> 00:46:09,759
Can you tell the court
of what that material consists?

803
00:46:09,760 --> 00:46:13,130
Erased emails sent by One-Way
and some received by them.

804
00:46:13,700 --> 00:46:18,010
Mr Arditti, if emails are erased
how can you pick them up?

805
00:46:18,670 --> 00:46:20,669
Nothing is ever truly erased,
Your Honour.

806
00:46:20,670 --> 00:46:22,779
It˙s indelibly printed on the ether.

807
00:46:22,780 --> 00:46:25,309
Anyone can pick up this material
and read it?

808
00:46:25,310 --> 00:46:28,958
With the technical know-how, sir,
if they can reconstruct them.

809
00:46:29,050 --> 00:46:31,920
And you can clearly
identify their origin?

810
00:46:32,420 --> 00:46:34,260
Oh, yes, from their ISP ident.

811
00:46:36,220 --> 00:46:38,259
Yeah, but where do they actually exist?

812
00:46:38,260 --> 00:46:40,790
Perhaps if I were to reveal
the information?

813
00:46:40,960 --> 00:46:42,040
Yeah.

814
00:46:42,290 --> 00:46:47,450
Mr Arditti, how many lost or erased
emails have you or your firm recovered?

815
00:46:48,230 --> 00:46:49,819
Thousands. It˙s what we do.

816
00:46:50,100 --> 00:46:52,239
In what circumstances do you do this?

817
00:46:52,240 --> 00:46:55,310
Mostly material lost by power cuts
and viruses.

818
00:46:55,810 --> 00:46:58,046
Sometimes people hit the wrong button.

819
00:46:58,210 --> 00:47:00,730
In your expert opinion, Mr Arditti,

820
00:47:01,180 --> 00:47:04,879
could you say whether the documents
deleted by One-Way and recovered by you

821
00:47:04,880 --> 00:47:06,704
had been deleted intentionally?

822
00:47:06,750 --> 00:47:10,927
My Lord, this witness cannot possibly
give an answer to such a question.

823
00:47:11,060 --> 00:47:13,296
He is an expert witness, Mrs Channing.

824
00:47:13,590 --> 00:47:16,310
He isn˙t an expert in mind-reading.

825
00:47:16,760 --> 00:47:19,937
He couldn˙t possibly know
the intentions of my clients.

826
00:47:20,130 --> 00:47:21,895
JO: I˙ll rephrase my question.

827
00:47:21,930 --> 00:47:24,754
Is it possible that even
an inexperienced IT user

828
00:47:24,870 --> 00:47:28,470
could have erased nine separate
but related emails by mistake?

829
00:47:29,040 --> 00:47:31,560
No, not even by block erasement.

830
00:47:33,380 --> 00:47:37,510
My Lord, under Rule 31-7
of the Civil Procedure Rules,

831
00:47:38,150 --> 00:47:40,520
defendants are under a duty to make

832
00:47:40,920 --> 00:47:45,097
a reasonable search for documents
which adversely affect their own case.

833
00:47:45,460 --> 00:47:47,402
It would seem they didn˙t search.

834
00:47:47,560 --> 00:47:50,520
They couldn˙t,
because the emails had been erased.

835
00:47:51,630 --> 00:47:54,513
Mrs Channing,
I˙m sure you˙ve advised your clients

836
00:47:54,770 --> 00:47:57,580
that Section 450
of the Companies Act 1985

837
00:47:58,040 --> 00:47:59,669
provides that a company officer

838
00:47:59,670 --> 00:48:02,420
who destroys, mutilates
or falsifies a document

839
00:48:02,880 --> 00:48:06,690
affecting the company˙s affairs
may be liable to prosecution?

840
00:48:07,280 --> 00:48:09,030
The directors of One-Way PLC

841
00:48:09,380 --> 00:48:12,919
are conversant with the laws governing
companies and are not in breach of them.

842
00:48:12,920 --> 00:48:14,920
We would argue in the alternative,

843
00:48:14,950 --> 00:48:19,009
were the documents deemed admissible,
as you seem determined they are.

844
00:48:19,090 --> 00:48:20,855
I˙m determining no such thing.

845
00:48:21,130 --> 00:48:22,859
I would contend there is no proof,

846
00:48:22,860 --> 00:48:25,499
they are or ever were
the company˙s documents.

847
00:48:25,500 --> 00:48:26,729
They have One-Way˙s logo

848
00:48:26,730 --> 00:48:30,083
and bear the name of
the Marketing Director, Max Solveigh.

849
00:48:31,370 --> 00:48:33,339
Anyone could have printed
these documents.

850
00:48:33,340 --> 00:48:36,870
First we˙d better establish
if these documents are authentic.

851
00:48:37,640 --> 00:48:41,450
Mr Arditti, can you trace
these documents back to the company?

852
00:48:42,050 --> 00:48:44,949
As if it had been a physical letter
on their stationery,

853
00:48:44,950 --> 00:48:48,419
using their old typewriters with the
director˙s signature at the end of it.

854
00:48:48,420 --> 00:48:51,759
Yes, but as Mrs Channing pointed out,
anybody could have sent such a document.

855
00:48:51,760 --> 00:48:55,159
If you compare these emails with those
sent by the research company earlier,

856
00:48:55,160 --> 00:48:58,219
you˙ll see the same phrasing,
the same use of jargon.

857
00:48:58,230 --> 00:49:01,300
Is Mr Arditti now a language expert?

858
00:49:02,940 --> 00:49:06,235
It might be useful, My Lord,
if we were to ask Mr Arditti

859
00:49:06,340 --> 00:49:08,239
how he came to the missing emails?

860
00:49:08,240 --> 00:49:09,829
Yes, all right. Mr Arditti?

861
00:49:11,910 --> 00:49:14,779
We had asked the research company,
Whitten and Malle, for them.

862
00:49:14,780 --> 00:49:17,680
They did the testing
for One-Way on the ZP-9.

863
00:49:18,180 --> 00:49:22,416
They refused to help because it was
part of their confidentiality clause.

864
00:49:22,420 --> 00:49:25,659
But it was later sent to us
by Whitten and Malle as a ghost,

865
00:49:25,660 --> 00:49:29,359
mistakenly attached to their email
response to some innocuous enquiries.

866
00:49:29,360 --> 00:49:31,529
My Lord, this is more
and more ludicrous.

867
00:49:31,530 --> 00:49:34,569
I would ask that the court reject
these documents as inadmissible.

868
00:49:34,570 --> 00:49:38,629
No, I˙m not inclined to without
further investigation of their source.

869
00:49:40,340 --> 00:49:43,399
First, I˙m going to consider them
over an adjournment

870
00:49:43,480 --> 00:49:45,209
to see if they are germane to the case.

871
00:49:45,210 --> 00:49:46,580
You would.

872
00:49:46,850 --> 00:49:48,020
USHER: All rise.

873
00:49:54,290 --> 00:49:58,059
- Well, we can see where this is going.
- I hope so, George, I do hope so.

874
00:49:58,060 --> 00:50:00,649
I daresay you˙ll decide that
in bed together.

875
00:50:01,060 --> 00:50:02,990
Oh, wouldn˙t that be nice?

876
00:50:03,330 --> 00:50:05,960
(PEOPLE CHATTERING)

877
00:50:12,670 --> 00:50:13,670
George.

878
00:50:13,870 --> 00:50:15,770
Ah. This is a surprise.

879
00:50:18,380 --> 00:50:20,679
Of course, I was hoping to hear
some of the argument.

880
00:50:20,680 --> 00:50:23,119
The Deed already suspects
some sort of government collusion

881
00:50:23,120 --> 00:50:24,249
to protect my client.

882
00:50:24,250 --> 00:50:28,133
The presence of the Secretary of State
in court would convince him.

883
00:50:28,220 --> 00:50:29,220
Neil.

884
00:50:29,860 --> 00:50:31,759
- Good to see you.
- Max, how are you?

885
00:50:31,760 --> 00:50:33,759
At this rate I˙m going to be
the marketing director

886
00:50:33,760 --> 00:50:35,937
of an insolvent mobile phone company.

887
00:50:35,960 --> 00:50:37,999
Our shares slipped
another two points today.

888
00:50:38,000 --> 00:50:39,200
Oh, it will stop.

889
00:50:40,230 --> 00:50:41,280
I promise you.

890
00:50:42,330 --> 00:50:45,930
Mrs Cooper, is it possible
to have a word with the judge?

891
00:50:46,540 --> 00:50:49,409
As a failed litigant,
I think you know better, Sergeant.

892
00:50:49,410 --> 00:50:50,780
It˙s not about my case.

893
00:50:52,010 --> 00:50:54,030
Well, I wouldn˙t hold your breath.

894
00:50:54,910 --> 00:50:56,840
Mrs Mills, might we have a word?

895
00:50:57,220 --> 00:50:59,985
If you˙re brief, Mr James.
I have a tuba lesson.

896
00:51:00,620 --> 00:51:02,689
We wondered if we might be
of any assistance

897
00:51:02,690 --> 00:51:05,559
Over yOur appearanCe
before the Disciplinary Tribunal?

898
00:51:05,560 --> 00:51:08,289
Why would you? It came from
your department in the beginning.

899
00:51:08,290 --> 00:51:10,529
We were compelled to act on a complaint.

900
00:51:10,530 --> 00:51:11,840
Then make it go away.

901
00:51:13,300 --> 00:51:16,477
That is almost entirely in the gift
of Mr Justice Deed.

902
00:51:19,000 --> 00:51:20,765
Shall we walk you to your car?

903
00:51:27,880 --> 00:51:31,351
The Brooklands case isn˙t coming back
this afternoon, Judge.

904
00:51:32,420 --> 00:51:34,350
Mr Brooklands had a fit.

905
00:51:34,690 --> 00:51:37,867
I thought he was on so many drugs
he could barely move.

906
00:51:40,490 --> 00:51:42,389
Is Mrs Mills going to win
the argument, Judge?

907
00:51:42,390 --> 00:51:43,859
No, I don˙t think so, Coop.

908
00:51:43,860 --> 00:51:46,272
Not if the emails
were obtained illegally.

909
00:51:46,300 --> 00:51:49,170
Oh, dear, that˙s more bad news for her.

910
00:51:49,630 --> 00:51:51,680
I heard Mr Justice Everard

911
00:51:52,040 --> 00:51:54,923
is presiding at her
Disciplinary Tribunal hearing.

912
00:51:56,040 --> 00:51:57,650
Where˙d you hear that?

913
00:51:58,640 --> 00:52:00,510
Mr Justice Everard˙s clerk.

914
00:52:04,450 --> 00:52:06,745
You could get us out of this, you know.

915
00:52:08,250 --> 00:52:10,959
The circuit administrator implied
that if you dropped the cell phone case

916
00:52:10,960 --> 00:52:13,196
my Disciplinary Tribunal would vanish.

917
00:52:13,360 --> 00:52:14,620
Now, that is illegal.

918
00:52:14,890 --> 00:52:17,159
I˙ll have him bounced
right out of the system.

919
00:52:17,160 --> 00:52:18,631
It˙s my word against his.

920
00:52:18,800 --> 00:52:20,929
And who˙d believe me,
especially if I lose the hearing?

921
00:52:20,930 --> 00:52:23,754
Jo, I told you
I˙d give evidence at the Tribunal.

922
00:52:23,970 --> 00:52:26,794
And what will you say?
That I wasn˙t in your bed?

923
00:52:27,540 --> 00:52:28,540
If necessary.

924
00:52:28,610 --> 00:52:31,509
Great, then they˙ll produce the photos
and prove you a liar.

925
00:52:31,510 --> 00:52:33,109
Don˙t even know
what˙s in the photos yet.

926
00:52:33,110 --> 00:52:35,300
George says they˙re of us in bed.

927
00:52:37,080 --> 00:52:39,845
Who˙s doing the hearing for you?
Is he any good?

928
00:52:41,320 --> 00:52:43,159
Eleven months this has been going on.

929
00:52:43,160 --> 00:52:45,350
- Finally, you ask.
- Si...

930
00:52:45,720 --> 00:52:47,559
What˙s his name?
Simon Norwalk, isn˙t it?

931
00:52:47,560 --> 00:52:49,400
He˙s got a good reputation.

932
00:52:49,730 --> 00:52:51,907
Providing he gives it full attention.

933
00:52:52,100 --> 00:52:53,329
If you are not going
to stop this hearing

934
00:52:53,330 --> 00:52:54,969
the less I have to do with you
at the moment...

935
00:52:54,970 --> 00:52:56,899
What do you want me to do?
Make a deal with Ian Rochester?

936
00:52:56,900 --> 00:52:58,239
Give the case to Legover?

937
00:52:58,240 --> 00:53:00,064
Fine, I˙ll make it all go away.

938
00:53:00,170 --> 00:53:02,310
I don˙t knowl

939
00:53:02,670 --> 00:53:05,109
I˙m not sure that˙s the best thing
for Diana Hulsey and her son.

940
00:53:05,110 --> 00:53:07,379
No, it˙s best for you, though, isn˙t it?

941
00:53:07,380 --> 00:53:09,209
You think that˙s taking responsibility?

942
00:53:09,210 --> 00:53:11,719
Oh, I see,
that˙s what it˙s all about, is it?

943
00:53:11,720 --> 00:53:14,073
Fine, I˙ll resign,
and we˙ll get married.

944
00:53:14,090 --> 00:53:16,443
Oh, grow up, Johnl
Right now I feel as if

945
00:53:16,620 --> 00:53:19,659
I am drowning and everything that you do
is just another stone in my pocket.

946
00:53:19,660 --> 00:53:22,760
Yeah, go on, walk out.
No, don˙tl Don˙t do that.

947
00:53:23,730 --> 00:53:24,810
Stop.

948
00:53:26,300 --> 00:53:29,429
Let˙s think about this logically,
there˙s bound to be a solution.

949
00:53:29,430 --> 00:53:31,980
Think about your dinner.
The oil˙s burning.

950
00:53:47,220 --> 00:53:50,470
Judge, my husband James Brooklands,
he˙s not faking it.

951
00:53:51,020 --> 00:53:52,759
Why do you assume I think he is?

952
00:53:52,760 --> 00:53:53,929
Well, I thought that...

953
00:53:53,930 --> 00:53:56,999
Really, you mustn˙t approach me
like this, Mrs Brooklands.

954
00:53:57,000 --> 00:54:00,280
He˙s such a decent man,
he˙s kind and generous.

955
00:54:03,900 --> 00:54:05,540
Members of the jury,

956
00:54:05,840 --> 00:54:08,664
this a trial unlike any
you˙ve come across before

957
00:54:09,040 --> 00:54:10,940
or are likely to again.

958
00:54:11,840 --> 00:54:16,620
You are not trying the defendant
as to his being guilty or not guilty.

959
00:54:18,320 --> 00:54:21,919
You are being asked to decide
whether he is capable of understanding

960
00:54:21,920 --> 00:54:23,626
what is unfolding before him.

961
00:54:24,690 --> 00:54:28,000
Our contention is that
he is incapable of understanding

962
00:54:28,530 --> 00:54:30,883
and therefore should not be in the dock.

963
00:54:31,100 --> 00:54:34,336
He should, as I hope you˙ll agree
after hearing evidence

964
00:54:34,830 --> 00:54:37,550
from our eminent psychiatrists,

965
00:54:38,000 --> 00:54:40,170
be in hospital receiving treatment.

966
00:54:41,370 --> 00:54:46,880
Dr Shapiro, are you professor
of psychiatry, King˙s College, London,

967
00:54:47,710 --> 00:54:50,110
and author of Uncognitive Mind?

968
00:54:51,120 --> 00:54:52,670
Yes, I am.

969
00:54:53,450 --> 00:54:56,350
Have you examined the defendant,
James Brooklands?

970
00:54:56,860 --> 00:54:59,743
SHAPIRO: Yes, at length
over the past four months.

971
00:54:59,990 --> 00:55:02,329
PETROS: Has he shown
any change in that period?

972
00:55:02,330 --> 00:55:05,020
He has. Unfortunately,
none for the better.

973
00:55:05,500 --> 00:55:07,795
He has withdrawn into a catatonic state

974
00:55:07,900 --> 00:55:10,548
where he has no recognition
of what is present

975
00:55:10,770 --> 00:55:11,790
and what is past.

976
00:55:12,000 --> 00:55:14,220
Can he function in this state?

977
00:55:14,610 --> 00:55:17,220
Tie his shoelaces,
shave, clean his teeth?

978
00:55:17,640 --> 00:55:19,340
He˙s not unconscious.

979
00:55:19,640 --> 00:55:23,510
Catatonic states merely cut off
conscious recognition or response.

980
00:55:24,120 --> 00:55:27,900
If you saw him in the street
and called, "James..."

981
00:55:32,060 --> 00:55:33,990
He wouldn˙t respond.

982
00:55:34,330 --> 00:55:35,410
I see.

983
00:55:37,460 --> 00:55:40,830
Dr Shapiro, if the defendant
were found guilty as charged

984
00:55:41,370 --> 00:55:42,830
and sent to prison,

985
00:55:43,130 --> 00:55:46,030
what in your opinion
would be his reaction?

986
00:55:46,540 --> 00:55:49,060
Because of the absence
of cognitive powers,

987
00:55:49,470 --> 00:55:52,279
it would make no difference
to his sense of well-being

988
00:55:52,280 --> 00:55:56,230
whether he was given a long prison
sentence or a complete discharge.

989
00:56:04,720 --> 00:56:06,150
DEED: Mr Gaydon?

990
00:56:08,990 --> 00:56:11,770
Could a patient
fake this illness, Doctor?

991
00:56:12,230 --> 00:56:15,169
- I˙ve never seen it faked.
- Will he recover, Doctor?

992
00:56:15,170 --> 00:56:17,420
If I may, My Lord, please.

993
00:56:18,340 --> 00:56:23,030
Dr Shapiro,
did you write a paper in 1995

994
00:56:23,780 --> 00:56:26,330
positing that any number
of mental patients

995
00:56:26,780 --> 00:56:28,300
were at any given time

996
00:56:28,610 --> 00:56:30,280
faking their illnesses?

997
00:56:30,620 --> 00:56:32,460
SHAPIRO: I don˙t think so.

998
00:56:35,450 --> 00:56:37,700
Is that a paper written by you?

999
00:56:38,090 --> 00:56:40,443
DEED: Are you exhibiting this,
Mr Gaydon?

1000
00:56:40,690 --> 00:56:42,680
GAYDON: I will be, My Lord.

1001
00:56:43,730 --> 00:56:44,930
(CHUCKLES)

1002
00:56:46,100 --> 00:56:49,820
That is my paper.
I have to confess to have been

1003
00:56:50,400 --> 00:56:54,669
heavily influenced at the time by the
then professor of forensic psychiatry

1004
00:56:54,670 --> 00:56:56,750
at Birmingham University.

1005
00:56:57,140 --> 00:56:59,540
Like him, have you known offenders

1006
00:56:59,980 --> 00:57:02,579
who, because they˙ve been
declared mentally ill,

1007
00:57:02,580 --> 00:57:05,816
have been allowed to shuffle away
from the Crown Courts?

1008
00:57:05,950 --> 00:57:09,640
Then to be released from
the mental hospital a few months later

1009
00:57:10,260 --> 00:57:12,070
completely cured and free?

1010
00:57:12,390 --> 00:57:14,970
Not with a catatonic condition.

1011
00:57:18,130 --> 00:57:22,320
When was Mr Brooklands last able
to give his defence team instruction?

1012
00:57:22,970 --> 00:57:26,839
He was barely able to instruct them
when I was first asked to examine him.

1013
00:57:26,840 --> 00:57:30,605
But he was, to all intents and purposes,
instructing his defence?

1014
00:57:31,180 --> 00:57:34,533
I doubt it. He can barely conduct
a coherent conversation.

1015
00:57:35,410 --> 00:57:36,410
Mmm.

1016
00:57:37,320 --> 00:57:39,690
Yet less than five weeks ago

1017
00:57:40,080 --> 00:57:43,740
he wrote a cheque in part payment
of defence expenses.

1018
00:57:44,320 --> 00:57:45,559
How could he do that, Doctor?

1019
00:57:45,560 --> 00:57:47,610
He can still write his name.

1020
00:57:47,960 --> 00:57:50,710
He doesn˙t know what he˙s writing.

1021
00:57:51,160 --> 00:57:55,160
Then it˙s fortunate for the defence team
that they put his chequebook

1022
00:57:55,330 --> 00:57:58,330
duly filled in with all the details
in front of him.

1023
00:57:58,400 --> 00:58:01,710
Would you confine yourself
to questions, Mr Gaydon?

1024
00:58:02,240 --> 00:58:06,160
I do apologise, My Lord.
I have no further questions.

1025
00:58:06,810 --> 00:58:09,222
DEED: Mr Petros,
do you want to come back?

1026
00:58:10,620 --> 00:58:13,649
Dr Shapiro, was there any process
of cognitive treatment

1027
00:58:13,650 --> 00:58:17,089
that Mr Brooklands underwent
during the time you˙ve been seeing him?

1028
00:58:17,090 --> 00:58:21,340
Yes, of course. He was encouraged
to partake in familiar functions.

1029
00:58:21,990 --> 00:58:24,800
Was signing a cheque
one of these functions?

1030
00:58:25,300 --> 00:58:28,300
Yes, it was part
of his cognitive behaviour therapy.

1031
00:58:31,940 --> 00:58:34,340
What is your prognosis
for Mr Brooklands?

1032
00:58:35,740 --> 00:58:38,839
It is hoped that with sustained
cognitive behaviour therapy

1033
00:58:38,840 --> 00:58:40,782
he will recover some recognition.

1034
00:58:40,810 --> 00:58:44,850
In what sort of time frame
might you expect a result?

1035
00:58:45,480 --> 00:58:49,930
Perhaps in two or three years.
It˙s hard to quantify.

1036
00:58:50,660 --> 00:58:52,689
It˙s not likely to happen suddenly?

1037
00:58:52,690 --> 00:58:55,279
I˙ve never known patients
to recover quickly.

1038
00:58:56,460 --> 00:58:59,460
Is this sort of treatment
available through the NHS?

1039
00:58:59,630 --> 00:59:02,260
Mr Brooklands
is being treated privately.

1040
00:59:02,730 --> 00:59:04,900
Oh, I see. Another cheque?

1041
00:59:06,270 --> 00:59:09,212
There is treatment available
through state medicine

1042
00:59:09,540 --> 00:59:13,540
but too many psychiatrists have jumped
on cognitive behaviour therapy

1043
00:59:13,580 --> 00:59:17,680
because it˙s inexpensive,
but they don˙t employ it properly.

1044
00:59:18,320 --> 00:59:21,630
Would it be injurious to Mr Brooklands

1045
00:59:22,150 --> 00:59:24,960
if he were to go into the witness box?

1046
00:59:25,460 --> 00:59:27,860
He simply wouldn˙t know why he˙s there.

1047
00:59:28,290 --> 00:59:29,550
DEED: I see.

1048
00:59:30,760 --> 00:59:34,300
All right, let us assume,
just assume for the moment,

1049
00:59:34,900 --> 00:59:38,270
that it was Mr Brooklands
who brought about the deaths

1050
00:59:38,800 --> 00:59:41,153
of this young mother
and her two children

1051
00:59:41,210 --> 00:59:43,110
and then fled the scene.

1052
00:59:43,440 --> 00:59:46,540
That the terrible regret
that he then suffered

1053
00:59:47,050 --> 00:59:49,830
caused him to lapse
into this catatonic state.

1054
00:59:51,080 --> 00:59:52,849
And then two or three years later

1055
00:59:52,850 --> 00:59:55,203
he emerges from
the psychiatric hospital,

1056
00:59:55,620 --> 00:59:58,310
your treatment having been successful.

1057
00:59:59,860 --> 01:00:03,096
What is it then that would
prevent him from lapsing back

1058
01:00:03,390 --> 01:00:04,940
into this catatonic state

1059
01:00:05,230 --> 01:00:08,229
when he˙s faced with
the prospect of standing trial?

1060
01:00:08,230 --> 01:00:12,239
Part of the treatment is to bring about
self-awareness in the patient.

1061
01:00:12,240 --> 01:00:15,652
The idea is to get him to face
all that he˙s escaping from.

1062
01:00:15,670 --> 01:00:16,809
Yes, yes, yes, I see,

1063
01:00:16,810 --> 01:00:20,320
but in the meanwhile
he˙s a risk to himself and others.

1064
01:00:20,880 --> 01:00:23,880
That is why
he˙s in a psychiatric hospital, My Lord.

1065
01:00:24,050 --> 01:00:28,330
Well, seems eminently sensible.

1066
01:00:30,420 --> 01:00:35,900
So I think for his own safety
and in view of the public outrage,

1067
01:00:36,730 --> 01:00:38,540
under the Mental Health Act,

1068
01:00:38,900 --> 01:00:41,970
perhaps I should commit
Mr Brooklands to Broadmoor.

1069
01:00:42,500 --> 01:00:43,610
No, no.

1070
01:00:46,040 --> 01:00:47,639
You can˙t, I˙m mentally ill.

1071
01:00:47,640 --> 01:00:49,009
PETROS: My Lord,
it˙s not a proper response.

1072
01:00:49,010 --> 01:00:51,579
Well, of course, you˙re quite right,
Mr Petros, it isn˙t.

1073
01:00:51,580 --> 01:00:53,409
Your client doesn˙t
seem to think so either.

1074
01:00:53,410 --> 01:00:56,881
Patients quite often have such
moments of lucidity, My Lord.

1075
01:00:57,080 --> 01:00:59,070
Your words, Doctor, not mine.

1076
01:01:03,150 --> 01:01:05,503
I think I˙m going to take a short break.

1077
01:01:05,860 --> 01:01:10,096
Members of the jury, why don˙t you
go and get yourselves a cup of coffee.

1078
01:01:12,560 --> 01:01:14,700
I wish to see counsel in chambers.

1079
01:01:32,220 --> 01:01:33,450
(KNOCK ON DOOR)

1080
01:01:53,600 --> 01:01:54,890
(EXHALES)

1081
01:01:56,870 --> 01:02:00,753
If you tell me, as counsel,
that you had no part in this conspiracy

1082
01:02:01,180 --> 01:02:02,533
to deceive the court...

1083
01:02:02,610 --> 01:02:04,720
- Judge.
- Mr Petros.

1084
01:02:06,280 --> 01:02:09,119
If as Queen˙s Counsel,
you tell me that you˙re blameless

1085
01:02:09,120 --> 01:02:12,250
then I must believe you
and there the matter will end,

1086
01:02:12,760 --> 01:02:15,996
provided we move swiftly
to the trial on the indictment.

1087
01:02:16,260 --> 01:02:19,129
My Lord, I˙m guided
entirely by the psychiatric team.

1088
01:02:19,130 --> 01:02:22,229
I˙m convinced the defendant followed
every word that went on

1089
01:02:22,230 --> 01:02:23,720
and so is this jury.

1090
01:02:24,000 --> 01:02:26,000
Now, we can continue and Mr Gaydon

1091
01:02:26,070 --> 01:02:29,569
can bring in expert witnesses
to challenge the highly paid team

1092
01:02:29,570 --> 01:02:31,530
the defence has amassed.

1093
01:02:32,280 --> 01:02:35,449
Or you can take instruction
from your perfectly lucid client,

1094
01:02:35,450 --> 01:02:39,290
we˙ll swear another jury
and proceed to trial on the indictment.

1095
01:02:43,090 --> 01:02:46,280
I˙m aware of no conspiracy
to deceive the court.

1096
01:02:46,790 --> 01:02:47,810
Fine.

1097
01:02:48,930 --> 01:02:53,401
I˙ll talk to the instructing solicitor
and seek instructions from the client.

1098
01:02:53,930 --> 01:02:55,010
Good.

1099
01:02:55,930 --> 01:02:57,300
(DOOR OPENS)

1100
01:03:04,540 --> 01:03:07,179
It doesn˙t matter that
you haven˙t been called upon

1101
01:03:07,180 --> 01:03:09,050
to deliberate in this instance.

1102
01:03:09,410 --> 01:03:13,330
Mr Brooklands˙ return to
lucidity has been accepted

1103
01:03:13,950 --> 01:03:16,060
by his lawyers as permanent.

1104
01:03:16,450 --> 01:03:19,862
So if you will go with
the jury bailiff for now. Thank you.

1105
01:03:25,430 --> 01:03:28,169
Mr Gaydon, I assume the prosecution
is ready to try the charge

1106
01:03:28,170 --> 01:03:29,280
on the indictment?

1107
01:03:29,530 --> 01:03:30,900
Yes, My Lord, we are.

1108
01:03:31,170 --> 01:03:34,139
With your leave, My Lord,
in light of the changed circumstances,

1109
01:03:34,140 --> 01:03:35,846
might we have an adjournment?

1110
01:03:36,070 --> 01:03:38,718
I don˙t really want this
to drag on. How long?

1111
01:03:39,010 --> 01:03:40,775
Two weeks would be sufficient.

1112
01:03:40,780 --> 01:03:42,120
Mr Harrison?

1113
01:03:42,410 --> 01:03:46,860
HARRISON: Three weeks would be
more convenient, My Lord. The 29th?

1114
01:03:47,550 --> 01:03:49,830
DEED: Yes, all right, 29th it is.

1115
01:03:50,250 --> 01:03:54,015
In the meanwhile, Mr Gaydon,
the CPS might reconsider the charge.

1116
01:03:54,490 --> 01:03:57,902
Causing death by dangerous
driving might more reasonably be

1117
01:03:57,930 --> 01:04:01,048
ratcheted up to manslaughter
through gross negligence.

1118
01:04:01,200 --> 01:04:04,339
My Lord, that seems hardly fair
to my client at this late stage.

1119
01:04:04,340 --> 01:04:06,669
And how fair would it seem
to the deceased˙s family

1120
01:04:06,670 --> 01:04:09,569
or to the general public,
if in the light of Mr Brooklands

1121
01:04:09,570 --> 01:04:12,923
being found guilty, I could
only sentence him to 10 years?

1122
01:04:13,310 --> 01:04:16,179
Now, if there is sufficient evidence
it seems reasonable that

1123
01:04:16,180 --> 01:04:18,533
a graver charge
be put on the indictment.

1124
01:04:18,820 --> 01:04:21,115
Then might the same bail terms be kept?

1125
01:04:21,150 --> 01:04:24,562
Mr Brooklands is currently in hospital
receiving treatment.

1126
01:04:24,590 --> 01:04:27,759
In the changed circumstances,
hospital isn˙t appropriate.

1127
01:04:27,760 --> 01:04:29,584
He will be remanded in custody.

1128
01:04:29,730 --> 01:04:32,629
I think there is a very
real risk that he˙ll abscond,

1129
01:04:32,630 --> 01:04:34,329
based on what I˙ve seen today.

1130
01:04:34,330 --> 01:04:36,507
No, Alexander, don˙t let this happen.

1131
01:04:36,700 --> 01:04:38,070
He can˙t do this to me.

1132
01:04:38,370 --> 01:04:42,290
Mr Brooklands, this miraculous recovery

1133
01:04:42,940 --> 01:04:46,570
is enough to make
true believers of us all.

1134
01:04:47,180 --> 01:04:49,449
Now, I˙m encouraged
that you now seem fit

1135
01:04:49,450 --> 01:04:51,519
to face up to your responsibilities.

1136
01:04:51,520 --> 01:04:54,873
This is a trait often lacking
in men who appear before me.

1137
01:04:55,090 --> 01:04:57,459
- Take him down.
- Alexander, this is not fair.

1138
01:04:57,460 --> 01:04:59,860
Do somethingl Clive, call someonel

1139
01:05:05,530 --> 01:05:09,280
Coop, get hold of Simon Norwalk for me,
would you, please?

1140
01:05:09,900 --> 01:05:11,890
He˙s Mrs Mills˙ barrister.

1141
01:05:12,240 --> 01:05:13,909
I˙d like to meet with him
as soon as possible.

1142
01:05:13,910 --> 01:05:14,910
Yeah.

1143
01:05:17,140 --> 01:05:20,070
Thank you for fitting me in
at such short notice.

1144
01:05:20,580 --> 01:05:23,620
Presumably you didn˙t
discuss this with Mrs Mills.

1145
01:05:24,120 --> 01:05:26,230
- No.
- She didn˙t want you called.

1146
01:05:28,090 --> 01:05:32,540
It is necessary
I point out the risk you run, Sir John,

1147
01:05:33,260 --> 01:05:35,780
in becoming a witness for Mrs Mills.

1148
01:05:36,190 --> 01:05:37,955
What risk is that, Mr Norwalk?

1149
01:05:38,100 --> 01:05:41,453
If she were to be found guilty
of professional misconduct,

1150
01:05:41,570 --> 01:05:43,500
it might damage your reputation.

1151
01:05:43,830 --> 01:05:47,080
Equally if I fail to take
responsibility for my part.

1152
01:05:47,610 --> 01:05:49,340
I have to be frank, sir.

1153
01:05:50,610 --> 01:05:53,220
Did you sleep with Mrs Mills?

1154
01:05:54,850 --> 01:05:56,050
No.

1155
01:05:57,450 --> 01:06:00,440
There appears to be evidence
to the contrary.

1156
01:06:01,420 --> 01:06:04,820
Photographs taken by
some peeping Tom. What˙s in it?

1157
01:06:05,360 --> 01:06:06,889
I˙m under a strict embargo

1158
01:06:06,890 --> 01:06:10,126
not to discuss disclosed documents
with other witnesses.

1159
01:06:12,100 --> 01:06:13,100
(CLEARING THROAT)

1160
01:06:13,160 --> 01:06:17,060
Jo got paralytic, I put her to bed.

1161
01:06:17,700 --> 01:06:19,400
In your bed, Judge?

1162
01:06:19,740 --> 01:06:23,490
Have you any idea how
judges˙ lodgings work, Mr Norwalk?

1163
01:06:24,080 --> 01:06:26,130
It˙s all about seniority.

1164
01:06:26,480 --> 01:06:29,449
The judge is practically
the sovereign where he sits.

1165
01:06:29,450 --> 01:06:32,349
If he has a guest,
then that guest has the next best room

1166
01:06:32,350 --> 01:06:34,449
and everybody else gets pushed down one.

1167
01:06:34,450 --> 01:06:38,900
No way was I going to bounce
everybody out of bed at 2 a.m.

1168
01:06:39,590 --> 01:06:41,810
Mrs Mills got put into my room.

1169
01:06:43,560 --> 01:06:45,559
The chairman of the Tribunal
is bound to ask,

1170
01:06:45,560 --> 01:06:48,340
even if counsel
for the committee doesn˙t,

1171
01:06:49,500 --> 01:06:53,950
did you sleep in the same bed
with Mrs Mills?

1172
01:06:55,440 --> 01:06:58,280
I certainly didn˙t sleep in a chair.

1173
01:07:00,880 --> 01:07:03,340
The photos are of you two in bed, sir.

1174
01:07:05,120 --> 01:07:08,149
The question we have to
consider is this, Mr Norwalk.

1175
01:07:08,150 --> 01:07:12,540
Would they dare not believe
a High Court judge?

1176
01:07:20,930 --> 01:07:22,829
Got an email from Sergeant Bridges.

1177
01:07:22,830 --> 01:07:25,290
Oh, he˙s very persistent.

1178
01:07:25,740 --> 01:07:28,069
This is got some information
about the cell phone case.

1179
01:07:28,070 --> 01:07:29,718
He wants to meet up with me.

1180
01:07:30,010 --> 01:07:33,670
Well, do you think you should?
I mean, not on your own.

1181
01:07:34,250 --> 01:07:36,709
Maybe I˙d better take
Row Colemore with me.

1182
01:07:36,710 --> 01:07:39,110
Yeah, I think that might be best, Judge.

1183
01:07:43,290 --> 01:07:46,540
- Do you think he˙s going to turn up?
- Because of you?

1184
01:07:49,090 --> 01:07:52,099
All we could find out about
the bomb hoaxer who cleared the court

1185
01:07:52,100 --> 01:07:53,959
was that the call was made
from a public box

1186
01:07:53,960 --> 01:07:56,490
outside the LCD building
in Victoria Street.

1187
01:07:56,600 --> 01:07:58,939
You might be right.
It could have been to stop you.

1188
01:07:58,940 --> 01:08:00,810
Oh, I hope not.

1189
01:08:01,170 --> 01:08:04,009
John, you˙re not backing off
now we˙ve gone through all this trouble.

1190
01:08:04,010 --> 01:08:05,590
Bomb hoaxes are bad news.

1191
01:08:05,880 --> 01:08:09,409
I just can˙t countenance an officer
of the court being responsible.

1192
01:08:09,410 --> 01:08:10,670
Mr Justice Deed?

1193
01:08:10,920 --> 01:08:12,410
Sergeant Bridges.

1194
01:08:13,420 --> 01:08:14,479
You know who I am?

1195
01:08:14,520 --> 01:08:17,180
I do. Deputy Assistant
Commissioner Colemore.

1196
01:08:17,660 --> 01:08:20,259
How did you obtain
a highly confidential email address?

1197
01:08:20,260 --> 01:08:23,070
Row, let˙s see
what Mr Bridges has to say.

1198
01:08:36,740 --> 01:08:39,960
If it˙s authentic,
it˙s pretty damning evidence.

1199
01:08:40,480 --> 01:08:42,320
How did you get this?

1200
01:08:42,650 --> 01:08:44,819
I hacked into
the One-Way computer system.

1201
01:08:44,820 --> 01:08:48,219
- In breach of the Data Protection Act.
- I˙m not an interested party.

1202
01:08:48,220 --> 01:08:51,059
You˙re here, Sergeant.
You˙ve been following the case in court.

1203
01:08:51,060 --> 01:08:53,389
I wanted you to see this, My Lord,
before you gave a ruling

1204
01:08:53,390 --> 01:08:55,329
on the on the inadmissibility
of the emails.

1205
01:08:55,330 --> 01:08:58,429
This email is inadmissible
because of how you obtained it.

1206
01:08:58,430 --> 01:09:00,010
I know that, My Lord.

1207
01:09:02,400 --> 01:09:04,510
Was there anything else there?

1208
01:09:04,870 --> 01:09:08,150
If I˙d found it,
that, too, would be inadmissible.

1209
01:09:13,410 --> 01:09:14,840
Thanks, Sergeant.

1210
01:09:20,480 --> 01:09:22,210
Seems like a good man, Row.

1211
01:09:22,520 --> 01:09:24,359
The police should hang onto him.

1212
01:09:24,360 --> 01:09:26,713
He˙s quitting his job on health grounds.

1213
01:09:26,920 --> 01:09:29,320
Perhaps he needs appreciating more.

1214
01:09:38,970 --> 01:09:41,050
My apologies for the late start.

1215
01:09:42,440 --> 01:09:45,460
- Ms Hulsey, how are you today?
- Fine, thank you.

1216
01:09:45,980 --> 01:09:46,980
Good.

1217
01:09:48,910 --> 01:09:52,734
Before I give my ruling on
the admissibility of the erased emails,

1218
01:09:53,050 --> 01:09:56,403
I want some further information
on how they were obtained.

1219
01:09:56,420 --> 01:10:00,185
Mr Arditti, would you mind
going back to the witness box, please?

1220
01:10:06,800 --> 01:10:08,859
Remember, you are still under oath.

1221
01:10:10,170 --> 01:10:16,120
Now, Mr Arditti, you said you were
accidentally sent erased emails.

1222
01:10:17,610 --> 01:10:22,500
Yes? So, how did you proceed
to recover further emails?

1223
01:10:23,250 --> 01:10:25,420
I mean, where were they exactly?

1224
01:10:25,820 --> 01:10:27,820
Think carefully before you answer.

1225
01:10:28,690 --> 01:10:31,089
With the internet service provider,
My Lord.

1226
01:10:31,090 --> 01:10:34,840
Yes. So, presumably you have
an arrangement with someone there

1227
01:10:35,460 --> 01:10:37,890
who allows you access to their dustbins?

1228
01:10:39,330 --> 01:10:40,759
You searched their records

1229
01:10:40,760 --> 01:10:43,390
in contravention
of the Data Protection Act.

1230
01:10:43,830 --> 01:10:45,569
Well, it˙s not quite like that, My Lord.

1231
01:10:45,570 --> 01:10:46,800
Oh, isn˙t it?

1232
01:10:47,970 --> 01:10:49,750
Go on, you can sit down.

1233
01:10:57,050 --> 01:10:59,849
- Yes, Mrs Channing.
- My Lord, I would like to argue

1234
01:10:59,850 --> 01:11:02,262
that this erased
email evidence be omitted

1235
01:11:02,620 --> 01:11:05,519
on account of how it was
obtained by illegal means.

1236
01:11:05,520 --> 01:11:07,109
I would refer Your Lordship

1237
01:11:07,160 --> 01:11:11,500
to the Attorney Generals
Reference number 3 of 1999.

1238
01:11:12,160 --> 01:11:15,429
You will see that in a case where
the police had illegally retained DNA

1239
01:11:15,430 --> 01:11:17,739
on a suspect,
the House of Lords said the fact

1240
01:11:17,740 --> 01:11:20,329
that the evidence
had been illegally acquired

1241
01:11:20,570 --> 01:11:24,394
could be a strong factor in
persuading a trialjudge to exclude it.

1242
01:11:24,440 --> 01:11:27,109
Yes, yes, yes, but the law
was amended by Section 82

1243
01:11:27,110 --> 01:11:29,579
of the Criminal Justice
and Police Act 2001,

1244
01:11:29,580 --> 01:11:32,849
and in any event that˙s all criminal law
and this is a civil case.

1245
01:11:32,850 --> 01:11:35,419
In that case, My Lord,
I would ask you to exclude the evidence

1246
01:11:35,420 --> 01:11:38,420
under the standard set
by the Civil Procedure Rules.

1247
01:11:38,520 --> 01:11:40,070
There was criminal intent.

1248
01:11:40,360 --> 01:11:44,129
The emails, which by my learned friend˙s
own admission were erased,

1249
01:11:44,130 --> 01:11:47,729
indicate that her clients
knowingly infringed the Companies Act.

1250
01:11:47,730 --> 01:11:49,690
It˙s punishable by a ￡10,000 fine

1251
01:11:50,030 --> 01:11:52,599
or six months in prison
on each count, My Lord.

1252
01:11:52,600 --> 01:11:53,770
This is ridiculous.

1253
01:11:54,000 --> 01:11:57,480
The law sometimes is, Mrs Channing.
Here, however, it˙s not.

1254
01:11:59,580 --> 01:12:00,920
Before I rule

1255
01:12:02,310 --> 01:12:06,620
I want to see what else there is
that may or may not have been erased.

1256
01:12:07,280 --> 01:12:10,519
My Lord, you have heard ample argument
to omit this evidence.

1257
01:12:10,520 --> 01:12:12,770
- I implore you to do so.
- No.

1258
01:12:13,590 --> 01:12:17,389
I˙m gonna make an order for the
claimant˙s legal team to make a search

1259
01:12:17,390 --> 01:12:21,200
of the computer records of One-Way PLC,
Whitten and Malle

1260
01:12:21,800 --> 01:12:25,640
and their respective internet
service providers if they so wish.

1261
01:12:26,240 --> 01:12:29,009
They must return here
with their findings next Monday.

1262
01:12:29,010 --> 01:12:32,179
I˙m obliged, My Lord,
but two days is so very little time.

1263
01:12:32,180 --> 01:12:35,479
They can work Saturday and Sunday,
Mrs Mills, and they better get started.

1264
01:12:35,480 --> 01:12:37,260
USHER: All rise.

1265
01:12:42,320 --> 01:12:44,859
Well, my only hope is that
they manage to drag him down

1266
01:12:44,860 --> 01:12:46,059
along with you tomorrow.

1267
01:12:46,060 --> 01:12:48,020
Who are they, George?

1268
01:12:49,090 --> 01:12:50,689
What does this mean, George?

1269
01:12:50,690 --> 01:12:53,899
It means that if you˙re hiding
anything, Max, they˙ll find it.

1270
01:12:53,900 --> 01:12:55,770
(PEOPLE MURMURING)

1271
01:12:58,600 --> 01:13:01,718
Off the record, John,
the Lord Chancellor has concerns

1272
01:13:01,970 --> 01:13:06,147
over the possibility of the mobile
phone case getting to a full hearing.

1273
01:13:06,640 --> 01:13:09,464
Has he seen the evidence, sir?
Because I haven˙t.

1274
01:13:09,480 --> 01:13:12,069
He was approached
by another cabinet minister

1275
01:13:12,480 --> 01:13:15,619
about the financial difficulties
the company could get into.

1276
01:13:15,620 --> 01:13:17,230
Neil Haughton, eh?

1277
01:13:17,920 --> 01:13:21,215
He feels it incumbent upon him
to try to find a solution.

1278
01:13:22,530 --> 01:13:24,354
And what˙s he suggesting, then?

1279
01:13:24,500 --> 01:13:28,169
Nothing that might benefit the claimant,
not with my ex-wife involved.

1280
01:13:28,170 --> 01:13:30,299
He suggested an out-of-court settlement.

1281
01:13:30,300 --> 01:13:33,069
Does he have influence
with One-Way PLC? Sit down, Ian.

1282
01:13:33,070 --> 01:13:34,240
Thank you.

1283
01:13:35,410 --> 01:13:39,109
Mimi, get offl You˙re not supposed
to be on the chairs. Come on.

1284
01:13:39,110 --> 01:13:40,920
- ROCHESTER: May I?
- Please.

1285
01:13:41,250 --> 01:13:42,980
Off you get.

1286
01:13:49,290 --> 01:13:53,330
The mobile phone company will
defend their position if they have to.

1287
01:13:54,590 --> 01:13:57,708
The damage that does
to the company and the country...

1288
01:13:58,730 --> 01:14:02,070
They do generate
￡10 to 15 billion of income,

1289
01:14:02,600 --> 01:14:04,953
all of which comes
back to Britain, John.

1290
01:14:05,170 --> 01:14:06,969
In this worsening economic climate

1291
01:14:06,970 --> 01:14:09,539
that is something any government
must take notice of.

1292
01:14:09,540 --> 01:14:13,070
Pursuit of a fair out-of-court
settlement is entirely proper.

1293
01:14:13,410 --> 01:14:15,705
But you˙re talking to the wrong person.

1294
01:14:17,610 --> 01:14:21,010
The defendants˙ legal representatives
are concerned

1295
01:14:21,550 --> 01:14:23,300
you are encouraging,

1296
01:14:23,750 --> 01:14:25,530
at the claimant˙s request,

1297
01:14:25,960 --> 01:14:27,300
a public trial.

1298
01:14:29,990 --> 01:14:34,350
Well, I do see this
as a very important test case.

1299
01:14:35,030 --> 01:14:36,960
This is perverse.

1300
01:14:37,330 --> 01:14:41,609
Thousands of livelihoods are dependent
upon the viability of this company.

1301
01:14:41,610 --> 01:14:44,979
And the thousands, possibly,
who might be damaged by the phones?

1302
01:14:44,980 --> 01:14:46,179
There˙s no evidence of that.

1303
01:14:46,180 --> 01:14:48,509
There never is when there are
vast sums of money involved.

1304
01:14:48,510 --> 01:14:52,350
Did you know in the 1700s
that gin was sold by pharmacists

1305
01:14:52,950 --> 01:14:54,489
as a health drink for women?

1306
01:14:54,490 --> 01:14:55,949
The government of the day

1307
01:14:55,950 --> 01:14:58,774
tacitly endorsed
an 8-million gallon consumption.

1308
01:14:59,190 --> 01:15:02,150
Fortunately the government today
show more concern.

1309
01:15:02,660 --> 01:15:06,955
Yes, over 100,000 people were killed
by alcohol in the UK alone last year.

1310
01:15:09,370 --> 01:15:11,980
Jo Mills isn˙t a fool, Ian.

1311
01:15:12,400 --> 01:15:16,636
If the settlement is honourable,
she˙ll advise the claimant to accept it.

1312
01:15:17,010 --> 01:15:18,410
- Mmm.
- Mmm.

1313
01:15:21,210 --> 01:15:24,960
(CLOCK CHIMING)

1314
01:15:40,500 --> 01:15:44,450
- How are you feeling?
- Angry. No, furious.

1315
01:15:50,610 --> 01:15:55,240
Oh, Mrs Deed. I do beg your pardon.
Mrs Mills.

1316
01:15:57,180 --> 01:16:02,130
Would you tell the committee what your
relationship is with Mr Justice Deed?

1317
01:16:02,890 --> 01:16:06,610
He was my pupil master,
then my lover, sometimes my mentor

1318
01:16:07,190 --> 01:16:09,060
and hopefully my friend.

1319
01:16:09,390 --> 01:16:12,760
Would you say
he was still an intimate friend?

1320
01:16:13,760 --> 01:16:16,100
We discuss intimate matters at times.

1321
01:16:16,530 --> 01:16:19,690
Do these discussions
translate to the sort of

1322
01:16:20,200 --> 01:16:23,390
physical intimacy
that leads you into his bed?

1323
01:16:25,080 --> 01:16:26,080
No.

1324
01:16:28,210 --> 01:16:31,858
That˙s not the evidence
three officers of the court have given.

1325
01:16:32,680 --> 01:16:36,370
They saw you kissing the judge
in an intimate way

1326
01:16:36,950 --> 01:16:40,640
around the 11th of June last year
when you were before him.

1327
01:16:42,730 --> 01:16:45,010
Why should this Tribunal believe you,

1328
01:16:45,400 --> 01:16:47,599
when you say
you weren˙t intimate with him?

1329
01:16:47,600 --> 01:16:50,440
We weren˙tl Not as you suggest.

1330
01:16:51,970 --> 01:16:55,559
The evidence informs of a somewhat
different behaviour pattern

1331
01:16:55,670 --> 01:16:58,860
with this judge
you again find yourself before.

1332
01:17:01,610 --> 01:17:05,610
Sir James, we do like to afford
witnesses the opportunity to respond.

1333
01:17:06,680 --> 01:17:09,798
Even though your statement
didn˙t call for a response.

1334
01:17:10,250 --> 01:17:12,000
Do you wish to reply?

1335
01:17:12,320 --> 01:17:14,589
This disciplinary hearing
has called into question

1336
01:17:14,590 --> 01:17:17,589
the standard of behaviour
by which I conduct myself,

1337
01:17:17,590 --> 01:17:20,140
both professionally and privately.

1338
01:17:21,100 --> 01:17:24,806
My behaviour is determined by
a personal sense of what I believe

1339
01:17:25,170 --> 01:17:27,510
to be right and proper and fair.

1340
01:17:27,940 --> 01:17:30,809
It doesn˙t allow one to collude
with criminal clients

1341
01:17:30,810 --> 01:17:32,109
whilst knowing that they are guilty,

1342
01:17:32,110 --> 01:17:34,699
nor does it allow one
to conspire with others

1343
01:17:34,880 --> 01:17:38,250
to undermine someone honourable,
decent and courageous.

1344
01:17:39,920 --> 01:17:42,549
I am comfortable
with the conduct of my relationships.

1345
01:17:42,550 --> 01:17:46,256
I have done nothing with which
I feel I need to reproach myself.

1346
01:17:47,720 --> 01:17:49,459
Beyond that, My Lord,
I have nothing to say.

1347
01:17:49,460 --> 01:17:53,880
I think you˙ve said quite enough.
Thank you, Mrs Mills.

1348
01:18:01,440 --> 01:18:03,676
We would like to call Mr Justice Deed.

1349
01:18:05,410 --> 01:18:07,179
Why didn˙t you tell me you
were calling him as a witness?

1350
01:18:07,180 --> 01:18:08,909
